00:30:18.480 --> 00:30:19.440
We can now go on.

00:30:19.440 --> 00:30:21.720
I have the distinct
pleasure of introducing

00:30:21.720 --> 00:30:24.240
two of our very own students--

00:30:24.240 --> 00:30:27.630
Maribel Gomez, a freshman
in chemical engineering,

00:30:27.630 --> 00:30:29.580
and Randall Pinkett,
a graduate student

00:30:29.580 --> 00:30:31.710
in media arts and sciences.

00:30:31.710 --> 00:30:35.010
Each will share and guide
us through some thoughts

00:30:35.010 --> 00:30:39.030
and reflections on the life
and legacy of Dr. King.

00:30:39.030 --> 00:30:41.754
So I'd like to
introduce Maribel Gomez.

00:30:41.754 --> 00:30:45.142
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:52.410 --> 00:30:53.980
GOMEZ: Good morning, everyone.

00:30:53.980 --> 00:30:56.550
My name is Maribel
Gomez, and I'm

00:30:56.550 --> 00:31:00.000
a member of the class of
2002, and I'm currently

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:03.660
pursuing a degree in
chemical engineering.

00:31:03.660 --> 00:31:08.370
In the 25 years since his death,
Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr,

00:31:08.370 --> 00:31:12.540
has become an American hero.

00:31:12.540 --> 00:31:16.050
As we remember the life
and dream of Dr. King,

00:31:16.050 --> 00:31:20.100
we remember the injustices
that Dr. King fought for,

00:31:20.100 --> 00:31:25.200
his fight for freedom, equality,
and dignity for all races

00:31:25.200 --> 00:31:29.070
and people, his many
speeches that awakened people

00:31:29.070 --> 00:31:31.740
to the horrors of
racial discrimination,

00:31:31.740 --> 00:31:35.460
and his work that spanned
and touched all minorities

00:31:35.460 --> 00:31:38.960
and motivated them to action.

00:31:38.960 --> 00:31:44.070
We remember, but we must
also act on his dream.

00:31:44.070 --> 00:31:47.910
To do this, we must
follow Dr. King's example

00:31:47.910 --> 00:31:50.880
and educate ourselves--

00:31:50.880 --> 00:31:54.900
educate ourselves on
the meaning and message

00:31:54.900 --> 00:32:00.660
of such a great educator
and those whom inspired him.

00:32:00.660 --> 00:32:06.060
Martin Luther King's ideas,
his call for racial equality,

00:32:06.060 --> 00:32:08.100
his faith in the
ultimate triumph

00:32:08.100 --> 00:32:13.050
of justice, his insistence
on the power of nonviolence

00:32:13.050 --> 00:32:16.420
to bring a major transformation
on American society

00:32:16.420 --> 00:32:21.150
are as vital and timely as ever.

00:32:21.150 --> 00:32:24.990
His life and teachings have
had a profound influence

00:32:24.990 --> 00:32:30.450
not only on Americans, but
of people of all nations.

00:32:30.450 --> 00:32:35.400
Dr. King said,
"Nothing in this world

00:32:35.400 --> 00:32:38.730
is more dangerous
than sincere ignorance

00:32:38.730 --> 00:32:42.090
and conscientious stupidity."

00:32:42.090 --> 00:32:46.200
Nothing in this world
is more dangerous

00:32:46.200 --> 00:32:50.820
than sincere ignorance and
conscientious stupidity.

00:32:50.820 --> 00:32:54.150
We are such a diverse,
multicultural nation

00:32:54.150 --> 00:32:56.790
that we can no longer
tolerate injustices

00:32:56.790 --> 00:32:59.620
based on the color of our skin.

00:32:59.620 --> 00:33:03.390
We must empower ourselves
with our knowledge

00:33:03.390 --> 00:33:09.150
and become educators ourselves
to those who are ignorant.

00:33:09.150 --> 00:33:12.090
History is still unfolding.

00:33:12.090 --> 00:33:16.800
Our actions today will be
the history of tomorrow.

00:33:16.800 --> 00:33:18.930
We must act accordingly.

00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:23.430
We must respond to
the words he preached.

00:33:23.430 --> 00:33:30.330
I quote, "We are faced with the
fact that tomorrow is today.

00:33:30.330 --> 00:33:35.370
We are confronted with
the fierce urgency of now.

00:33:35.370 --> 00:33:38.510
In this unfolding conundrum
of life and history,

00:33:38.510 --> 00:33:42.180
there is such a thing
as being too late.

00:33:42.180 --> 00:33:44.550
Let us not be too late.

00:33:44.550 --> 00:33:48.600
Let us not be too late."

00:33:48.600 --> 00:33:52.830
Let us all live and act
by the words of Dr. King.

00:33:52.830 --> 00:33:57.420
Let us educate those around us
of the joys of living together

00:33:57.420 --> 00:34:01.410
in harmony, and let
him continue to inspire

00:34:01.410 --> 00:34:06.570
us to perform extraordinary acts
of courage and perseverance,

00:34:06.570 --> 00:34:08.580
just like he did
when he ignited one

00:34:08.580 --> 00:34:12.389
of the most influential civil
rights and economic movements

00:34:12.389 --> 00:34:14.566
of the 20th century.

00:34:14.566 --> 00:34:16.440
I'd like to leave with
Dr. King's quote again

00:34:16.440 --> 00:34:18.090
as a reminder.

00:34:18.090 --> 00:34:21.449
"Nothing in this world
is more dangerous

00:34:21.449 --> 00:34:25.199
than sincere ignorance and
conscientious stupidity."

00:34:25.199 --> 00:34:26.189
Thank you.

00:34:26.189 --> 00:34:29.619
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:42.860 --> 00:34:44.900
SMITH: Thank you very,
very, very much, Maribel,

00:34:44.900 --> 00:34:48.810
for those inspiring and
thought-provoking remarks.

00:34:48.810 --> 00:34:51.600
We will now proceed with
Mr. Randall Pinkett.

00:34:51.600 --> 00:34:52.100
Randall?

00:35:07.140 --> 00:35:08.349
PINKETT: Thank you, Shana.

00:35:08.349 --> 00:35:08.890
Good morning.

00:35:12.170 --> 00:35:17.240
Somewhere I once heard that
the great figures among us

00:35:17.240 --> 00:35:23.210
have been ordinary people that
have done extraordinary things.

00:35:23.210 --> 00:35:26.540
The Reverend Dr.
Martin Luther King, Jr,

00:35:26.540 --> 00:35:29.280
did extraordinary things.

00:35:29.280 --> 00:35:30.870
But at the same time--

00:35:30.870 --> 00:35:33.200
and I don't think I diminish
Dr. King's great legacy

00:35:33.200 --> 00:35:35.090
by saying this--

00:35:35.090 --> 00:35:38.960
he was an ordinary man,
an ordinary man that

00:35:38.960 --> 00:35:43.410
did ordinary things
until December 1, 1955,

00:35:43.410 --> 00:35:45.470
when Rosa Parks
refused to give up

00:35:45.470 --> 00:35:48.380
her seat on a bus in
Montgomery, Alabama,

00:35:48.380 --> 00:35:53.120
and Dr. King, at the age
of 26 and just barely out

00:35:53.120 --> 00:35:57.710
of graduate school, was placed
at a crossroad along the path

00:35:57.710 --> 00:36:00.660
that constitutes human history.

00:36:00.660 --> 00:36:04.940
It was then that this
man, this ordinary man,

00:36:04.940 --> 00:36:10.880
became a great figure and
did extraordinary things.

00:36:10.880 --> 00:36:14.270
Robert Frost wrote, "Two
roads diverged in a wood,

00:36:14.270 --> 00:36:17.000
and I, I took the
one less traveled by.

00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:19.780
And that has made
all the difference."

00:36:19.780 --> 00:36:22.070
But in recognition
of Dr. King's legacy,

00:36:22.070 --> 00:36:24.590
I pose the following question.

00:36:24.590 --> 00:36:28.960
What about when
there is no road?

00:36:28.960 --> 00:36:29.960
Hmm?

00:36:29.960 --> 00:36:34.430
What about when there is
no road, no path to follow?

00:36:34.430 --> 00:36:36.740
Of the many things I've
learned from Dr. King's life,

00:36:36.740 --> 00:36:39.500
high among them is the
notion that each of us,

00:36:39.500 --> 00:36:43.070
in our own unique way, is
embarking on a new path

00:36:43.070 --> 00:36:45.470
with each passing
moment, venturing

00:36:45.470 --> 00:36:49.312
into uncharted territory.

00:36:49.312 --> 00:36:51.770
And while there are points and
places and moments and times

00:36:51.770 --> 00:36:56.120
where our paths cross and
intersect and intertwine,

00:36:56.120 --> 00:36:59.420
no two paths are alike.

00:36:59.420 --> 00:37:01.010
The honorable Mr.
Mfume has walked

00:37:01.010 --> 00:37:05.030
along a path, his own path.

00:37:05.030 --> 00:37:08.680
Our honorees today-- Mr.
Williamson, Ms. [INAUDIBLE],,

00:37:08.680 --> 00:37:12.530
and Professor Slocum-- have
walked along their own paths.

00:37:12.530 --> 00:37:14.450
And the Reverend Dr.
Martin Luther King, Jr,

00:37:14.450 --> 00:37:17.720
walked along his path.

00:37:17.720 --> 00:37:21.320
So I ask the question once
again, ladies and gentlemen.

00:37:21.320 --> 00:37:25.580
What about when
there is no road?

00:37:25.580 --> 00:37:27.470
The answer?

00:37:27.470 --> 00:37:32.780
The road is made as one walks.

00:37:32.780 --> 00:37:38.000
The road is made as one walks.

00:37:38.000 --> 00:37:40.340
Honoring the life and
legacy of Dr. King

00:37:40.340 --> 00:37:43.460
means remembering that just
as he walked along his path

00:37:43.460 --> 00:37:46.610
and was able to do great
things, we must walk along

00:37:46.610 --> 00:37:50.960
our path knowing that we,
too, can do great things,

00:37:50.960 --> 00:37:54.920
because if we put Dr. King
too high on a pedestal,

00:37:54.920 --> 00:37:57.050
if we allow his
accomplishments to in some way

00:37:57.050 --> 00:38:00.440
become unachievable
or unattainable,

00:38:00.440 --> 00:38:02.270
then not only do
we do a disservice

00:38:02.270 --> 00:38:04.820
to his life and
his legacy, but we

00:38:04.820 --> 00:38:09.140
diminish our own capacity
as ordinary people

00:38:09.140 --> 00:38:12.590
to do extraordinary things.

00:38:12.590 --> 00:38:15.666
God bless you, and God
bless you, Dr. King.

00:38:15.666 --> 00:38:19.138
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:40.480 --> 00:38:43.870
SMITH: Once again, I'd like to
thank both Maribel and Randall

00:38:43.870 --> 00:38:48.790
for sharing their thoughts
and remarks on Dr. King.

00:38:48.790 --> 00:38:50.770
I hope that everyone
here has had a chance

00:38:50.770 --> 00:38:57.100
to really take in what was said
and reflect for themselves.

00:38:57.100 --> 00:39:02.380
I would like to now introduce
the 15th President of MIT, Dr.

00:39:02.380 --> 00:39:05.380
