00:41:26.940 --> 00:41:31.020
I think it's fair to say that
MIT and the city of Cambridge

00:41:31.020 --> 00:41:35.280
are on a shared journey, and the
ways in which we work together

00:41:35.280 --> 00:41:38.550
in education, development,
and civic affairs

00:41:38.550 --> 00:41:42.240
is something that gives me
great confidence in our future.

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:46.530
It's good to have all of
you here this morning.

00:41:46.530 --> 00:41:52.260
Just four years ago, the
keynote speaker for this event

00:41:52.260 --> 00:41:58.080
was Judge A. Leon Higginbotham,
Jr. This past year,

00:41:58.080 --> 00:42:00.900
after a half century
of service as one

00:42:00.900 --> 00:42:04.380
of our nation's foremost
jurists, scholars,

00:42:04.380 --> 00:42:08.010
and advocates for civil
rights, Judge Higginbotham

00:42:08.010 --> 00:42:10.230
was taken from us.

00:42:10.230 --> 00:42:13.200
I could not let
this occasion pass

00:42:13.200 --> 00:42:19.410
without saying a few words about
this truly extraordinary man.

00:42:19.410 --> 00:42:23.610
Leon Higginbotham was a
hero for many Americans.

00:42:23.610 --> 00:42:26.580
Here at MIT, we will
forever be grateful

00:42:26.580 --> 00:42:30.930
for his compelling and
effective advocacy on our behalf

00:42:30.930 --> 00:42:35.460
during what became known
as the overlap case.

00:42:35.460 --> 00:42:39.050
As many in this room
remember, in 1991,

00:42:39.050 --> 00:42:40.860
the United States
Justice Department

00:42:40.860 --> 00:42:44.190
brought an antitrust suit
against MIT and against the Ivy

00:42:44.190 --> 00:42:47.160
League universities,
alleging collusion

00:42:47.160 --> 00:42:52.030
in the way in which we award
financial aid to our students.

00:42:52.030 --> 00:42:53.880
We argued that our
practices sought

00:42:53.880 --> 00:42:58.290
to ensure that students would
be awarded financial aid solely

00:42:58.290 --> 00:43:01.140
on the basis of
their financial need

00:43:01.140 --> 00:43:04.230
and that different schools would
not get into financial bidding

00:43:04.230 --> 00:43:06.750
wars for students.

00:43:06.750 --> 00:43:08.730
Judge Higginbotham,
who had recently

00:43:08.730 --> 00:43:13.770
retired from the bench, believed
our case was so compelling

00:43:13.770 --> 00:43:18.540
that he volunteered to present
pro-bono the amici briefs

00:43:18.540 --> 00:43:21.850
on our behalf at
the appellate court.

00:43:21.850 --> 00:43:23.670
A staunch and
effective supporter

00:43:23.670 --> 00:43:26.280
of affirmative
action and diversity,

00:43:26.280 --> 00:43:29.160
Leon Higginbotham argued
that the principles at stake

00:43:29.160 --> 00:43:32.970
in the overlap case had to
do with both public policy

00:43:32.970 --> 00:43:37.380
and ethical norms, and
one of those norms,

00:43:37.380 --> 00:43:42.180
he said, and I quote here, was
that "those people who are poor

00:43:42.180 --> 00:43:45.810
but who are talented should
have the same opportunity

00:43:45.810 --> 00:43:49.080
to have an entry into
these great universities

00:43:49.080 --> 00:43:51.360
as did the Kennedys
and the Rockefellers

00:43:51.360 --> 00:43:54.780
and the Cabots and the Lodges."

00:43:54.780 --> 00:43:57.210
One of the reasons he
worked so hard to preserve

00:43:57.210 --> 00:43:59.490
the practice of
need-blind admission

00:43:59.490 --> 00:44:02.970
and need-based financial
aid was his recognition

00:44:02.970 --> 00:44:05.940
that this policy was
a blessing not only

00:44:05.940 --> 00:44:08.770
for those who receive
the opportunity,

00:44:08.770 --> 00:44:12.330
but for the larger
society as well.

00:44:12.330 --> 00:44:15.540
After he had made his argument
to the appellate court,

00:44:15.540 --> 00:44:19.200
an argument that prevailed,
Judge Higginbotham

00:44:19.200 --> 00:44:21.270
got a letter from
an antitrust lawyer

00:44:21.270 --> 00:44:24.600
who had heard his
presentation to the court.

00:44:24.600 --> 00:44:28.110
That lawyer observed that
in the future, quote,

00:44:28.110 --> 00:44:32.040
"many poor white kids will
get into Ivy League schools,

00:44:32.040 --> 00:44:35.400
and they will not recognize that
this option was made possible

00:44:35.400 --> 00:44:38.480
because of the arguments
of a black lawyer

00:44:38.480 --> 00:44:43.260
and the Congressional Black
Caucus made on behalf of MIT

00:44:43.260 --> 00:44:47.830
for all of the poor
kids of America."

00:44:47.830 --> 00:44:52.890
As Judge Higginbotham reminded
us when he spoke here in 1995,

00:44:52.890 --> 00:44:54.930
we meet here today
to acknowledge

00:44:54.930 --> 00:44:59.160
that Martin Luther King's legacy
was not merely to black people

00:44:59.160 --> 00:45:00.780
or to brown people.

00:45:00.780 --> 00:45:05.940
His legacy was one that
embraced all people.

00:45:05.940 --> 00:45:08.520
This is perhaps the
least understood and most

00:45:08.520 --> 00:45:12.180
misrepresented aspect of the
ongoing debate over policies

00:45:12.180 --> 00:45:15.540
designed to promote more
equal opportunity and greater

00:45:15.540 --> 00:45:18.540
diversity in American society.

00:45:18.540 --> 00:45:21.150
Leon Higginbotham knew
that society could not

00:45:21.150 --> 00:45:24.960
progress unless it
progressed as a whole,

00:45:24.960 --> 00:45:29.100
with no one left
behind or pushed aside.

00:45:29.100 --> 00:45:33.150
Today, this fundamental policy,
this fundamental principle,

00:45:33.150 --> 00:45:39.300
is again under direct assault.
Across America, the old cry

00:45:39.300 --> 00:45:41.340
of "your gain is
my loss" is heard

00:45:41.340 --> 00:45:44.700
on college campuses,
corporate boardrooms,

00:45:44.700 --> 00:45:47.040
and the halls of Congress.

00:45:47.040 --> 00:45:51.030
It is a matter of profound and
disturbing irony that at a time

00:45:51.030 --> 00:45:55.860
of unparalleled prosperity, many
of those who have the most--

00:45:55.860 --> 00:45:58.830
the most wealth, the most
access, the most skills,

00:45:58.830 --> 00:46:00.360
the most power--

00:46:00.360 --> 00:46:03.000
seem to be more
reluctant than ever

00:46:03.000 --> 00:46:08.040
to invest those resources in
a strong and just society.

00:46:08.040 --> 00:46:11.820
In order to bring that strength
and justice to our society,

00:46:11.820 --> 00:46:14.880
all of us must continue
to think on and speak out

00:46:14.880 --> 00:46:18.690
about the issue of
race-sensitive admissions

00:46:18.690 --> 00:46:21.720
in American higher education.

00:46:21.720 --> 00:46:25.980
Since 1978, our colleges
and universities

00:46:25.980 --> 00:46:29.620
have designed and administered
admissions policies

00:46:29.620 --> 00:46:32.410
under the guidance of
the Bakke decision,

00:46:32.410 --> 00:46:36.940
which was forged in the Supreme
Court by Thurgood Marshall.

00:46:36.940 --> 00:46:39.310
This guidance is clear.

00:46:39.310 --> 00:46:43.150
Race may be one of
several factors considered

00:46:43.150 --> 00:46:47.560
when we select students for
admissions to our campuses.

00:46:47.560 --> 00:46:52.030
This simple statement, in my
view, is the appropriate one.

00:46:52.030 --> 00:46:55.000
Under it, as Bill Bowen
and Derek Bok have clearly

00:46:55.000 --> 00:46:58.930
demonstrated in their recent
book, The Shape of the River,

00:46:58.930 --> 00:47:02.500
our institutions have
contributed substantially

00:47:02.500 --> 00:47:05.860
to the establishment of a
strong black middle class,

00:47:05.860 --> 00:47:08.800
and therefore, to the
strengthening of America

00:47:08.800 --> 00:47:13.330
as a whole, but the quest
is far from complete.

00:47:13.330 --> 00:47:16.360
Today, through referenda
such as Proposition 209

00:47:16.360 --> 00:47:19.300
in California,
Initiative Measure

00:47:19.300 --> 00:47:23.080
200 in the state of
Washington, public universities

00:47:23.080 --> 00:47:25.690
are losing their
ability to utilize

00:47:25.690 --> 00:47:28.750
race-sensitive admissions
or even the ability

00:47:28.750 --> 00:47:31.360
to mount outreach
programs to minority

00:47:31.360 --> 00:47:35.470
youngsters in primary
and secondary schools.

00:47:35.470 --> 00:47:37.690
From MIT'S point of
view, more dangerous

00:47:37.690 --> 00:47:42.070
still are successful attacks
in the courts, especially

00:47:42.070 --> 00:47:45.400
the Hopwood case dealing with
admission to the University

00:47:45.400 --> 00:47:47.950
of Texas Law School.

00:47:47.950 --> 00:47:50.500
A case involving
undergraduate admissions

00:47:50.500 --> 00:47:54.430
at the University of Michigan
currently pending in the courts

00:47:54.430 --> 00:47:58.030
may be the most
important to date.

00:47:58.030 --> 00:48:01.090
Now, many argue on behalf
of these referenda and court

00:48:01.090 --> 00:48:04.150
challenges, and do
so in good faith,

00:48:04.150 --> 00:48:06.640
that it is discriminatory
to make race

00:48:06.640 --> 00:48:09.490
a consideration in
admissions and that students

00:48:09.490 --> 00:48:14.230
should be admitted only on
what they consider to be merit.

00:48:14.230 --> 00:48:17.680
In my view, there are two,
quite elementary flaws

00:48:17.680 --> 00:48:19.240
in this argument.

00:48:19.240 --> 00:48:22.420
First, we have
laid upon the table

00:48:22.420 --> 00:48:27.310
several factors, things such
as grades, ranks in class,

00:48:27.310 --> 00:48:31.490
test scores, geographic
distribution, breadth

00:48:31.490 --> 00:48:34.030
of interest, unusual
accomplishments

00:48:34.030 --> 00:48:38.800
outside the classroom,
race, economic status,

00:48:38.800 --> 00:48:43.060
international mixture,
and so forth and so on.

00:48:43.060 --> 00:48:47.950
Those who challenge us reach
out and remove from the table

00:48:47.950 --> 00:48:50.770
one and only one factor--

00:48:50.770 --> 00:48:55.770
race-- and say, thou
shalt not consider this.

00:48:55.770 --> 00:48:58.480
Second, their
underlying assumption

00:48:58.480 --> 00:49:00.730
is that we can
accurately measure

00:49:00.730 --> 00:49:03.370
specific quality
of our applicants

00:49:03.370 --> 00:49:06.130
by a simple number or two.

00:49:06.130 --> 00:49:08.320
They seem to seek a world
in which we are each

00:49:08.320 --> 00:49:13.390
ranked at age 18 by some easy
indicator like an SAT score

00:49:13.390 --> 00:49:18.080
which thereby determines
our breadth of opportunity.

00:49:18.080 --> 00:49:22.360
We believe that building a
class for a great university

00:49:22.360 --> 00:49:25.630
by using a range of
factors promotes a better

00:49:25.630 --> 00:49:29.170
educational experience
for all of our students,

00:49:29.170 --> 00:49:31.240
and that it
increases our ability

00:49:31.240 --> 00:49:35.590
to contribute to building the
strong, coherent, productive

00:49:35.590 --> 00:49:40.480
society this nation will
need in the next century.

00:49:40.480 --> 00:49:42.490
The logic underlying
this belief,

00:49:42.490 --> 00:49:47.980
to use a favorite MIT phrase,
is intuitively obvious,

00:49:47.980 --> 00:49:52.300
yet far too many of us still
cannot seem to understand

00:49:52.300 --> 00:49:56.830
the old patterns of exclusion
and separation did not work

00:49:56.830 --> 00:49:59.860
and must not be restored.

00:49:59.860 --> 00:50:02.530
They don't work for
society, and they really

00:50:02.530 --> 00:50:04.870
don't work for individuals.

00:50:04.870 --> 00:50:07.900
To the contrary,
individual fulfillment

00:50:07.900 --> 00:50:10.630
is enabled and enhanced
by participation

00:50:10.630 --> 00:50:12.820
in the larger community.

00:50:12.820 --> 00:50:16.540
Here on our campus, the recently
issued report of the Task Force

00:50:16.540 --> 00:50:19.570
on Student Life and
Learning reminds us

00:50:19.570 --> 00:50:22.660
that excellence in
research and education

00:50:22.660 --> 00:50:25.760
arise not only from
individual efforts,

00:50:25.760 --> 00:50:30.460
but also from commingling of
diverse ideas, perspectives,

00:50:30.460 --> 00:50:34.180
challenge of competition and
of teamwork, and the support

00:50:34.180 --> 00:50:36.640
of a nurturing community.

00:50:36.640 --> 00:50:39.730
The message underlying the
task force's recommendation

00:50:39.730 --> 00:50:44.970
is that MIT must become more
than just the sum of its parts,

00:50:44.970 --> 00:50:47.890
and in order to educate a
new generation of leaders

00:50:47.890 --> 00:50:52.330
for an increasingly
interconnected global society,

00:50:52.330 --> 00:50:55.480
we must, by our own
example, show students

00:50:55.480 --> 00:50:58.690
how to function,
communicate, and thrive

00:50:58.690 --> 00:51:01.330
in a diverse environment.

00:51:01.330 --> 00:51:04.390
One promising effort in
community-building at MIT

00:51:04.390 --> 00:51:07.150
is occurring in our
facilities department

00:51:07.150 --> 00:51:11.050
and on our campus police force,
where working and managing

00:51:11.050 --> 00:51:14.200
in a diverse environment
is made an explicit part

00:51:14.200 --> 00:51:17.290
of each person's
career development.

00:51:17.290 --> 00:51:19.450
While the program
is relatively new,

00:51:19.450 --> 00:51:22.740
early evidence suggests
that the direct attention

00:51:22.740 --> 00:51:26.540
to training and evaluation
and managing such issues

00:51:26.540 --> 00:51:30.680
can improve communication,
productivity, and employee

00:51:30.680 --> 00:51:33.830
satisfaction for everyone.

00:51:33.830 --> 00:51:36.320
Of course, many of the
same pressures and problems

00:51:36.320 --> 00:51:39.290
that affect our larger
society remain part

00:51:39.290 --> 00:51:42.360
of our everyday life at MIT.

00:51:42.360 --> 00:51:44.090
We're still only
at the beginning

00:51:44.090 --> 00:51:46.700
of a long and difficult journey.

00:51:46.700 --> 00:51:51.320
I hope, however, that even
in times of controversy,

00:51:51.320 --> 00:51:54.020
we hold onto the
example of such leaders

00:51:54.020 --> 00:51:57.080
as Dr. King and
Judge Higginbotham--

00:51:57.080 --> 00:52:01.580
remind us that our journey
cannot be completed alone.

00:52:01.580 --> 00:52:05.100
Unless all of us
help and each of us--

00:52:05.100 --> 00:52:08.720
unless all of us help
each to make our own way,

00:52:08.720 --> 00:52:11.180
we will never realize
the highest potential

00:52:11.180 --> 00:52:14.270
we have for excellence
and achievement.

00:52:14.270 --> 00:52:16.460
In designing our
admissions policies,

00:52:16.460 --> 00:52:20.030
in shaping our curricula,
and advising our students

00:52:20.030 --> 00:52:22.340
and working together,
we must never

00:52:22.340 --> 00:52:24.230
lose sight of the
truth expressed

00:52:24.230 --> 00:52:27.620
in these words written
by James Taylor

00:52:27.620 --> 00:52:30.650
to honor the memory of Dr. King.

00:52:30.650 --> 00:52:35.390
"We are bound together by
the tasks that lie before us

00:52:35.390 --> 00:52:37.830
and the road that lies ahead.

00:52:37.830 --> 00:52:40.970
We are bound, and we are bound."

00:52:40.970 --> 00:52:44.060
Thank you again very much
for being here this morning

00:52:44.060 --> 00:52:46.740
and for sharing this small
part of a great journey.

00:52:46.740 --> 00:52:47.240
Thank you.

00:52:47.240 --> 00:52:50.144
[APPLAUSE]

00:52:55.960 --> 00:53:00.070
And now, it's my
pleasure and honor

00:53:00.070 --> 00:53:03.400
to introduce this
morning's keynote speaker,

00:53:03.400 --> 00:53:10.000
the honorable Kweisi Mfume,
president and CEO of the NAACP.

00:53:10.000 --> 00:53:13.420
This annual ceremony
commemorating Dr. Martin Luther

00:53:13.420 --> 00:53:18.010
King, Jr, has been an important
feature of the MIT calendar

00:53:18.010 --> 00:53:20.650
for the last quarter century.

00:53:20.650 --> 00:53:25.060
Over that time, we have been
graced with many fine speakers,

00:53:25.060 --> 00:53:27.820
each of whom has made
a unique contribution

00:53:27.820 --> 00:53:30.760
to the history and
the cumulative impact

00:53:30.760 --> 00:53:32.980
of this event.

00:53:32.980 --> 00:53:36.190
The experience and achievements
of this year's keynote speaker,

00:53:36.190 --> 00:53:39.850
however, are so
impressive and so relevant

00:53:39.850 --> 00:53:42.820
that it would be difficult to
conceive of a better or more

00:53:42.820 --> 00:53:45.220
welcome choice.

00:53:45.220 --> 00:53:48.880
His own life journey is witness
to the transforming influence

00:53:48.880 --> 00:53:52.600
of determination,
education, and vision

00:53:52.600 --> 00:53:56.620
in the life of an
individual and to society.

00:53:56.620 --> 00:53:59.050
Raised in Baltimore's
inner city,

00:53:59.050 --> 00:54:02.250
he graduated from
Morgan State University

00:54:02.250 --> 00:54:03.880
and from the Johns
Hopkins School

00:54:03.880 --> 00:54:06.520
of International Studies.

00:54:06.520 --> 00:54:10.150
He then began his political
career back on home ground

00:54:10.150 --> 00:54:12.970
as a Baltimore city councilman.

00:54:12.970 --> 00:54:16.540
In 1986, he was
elected to Congress,

00:54:16.540 --> 00:54:19.900
where he served for 10
years as representative

00:54:19.900 --> 00:54:22.750
of Maryland's seventh district.

00:54:22.750 --> 00:54:25.360
In the course of his
Congressional career,

00:54:25.360 --> 00:54:27.670
Mr. Mfume was a
powerful advocate

00:54:27.670 --> 00:54:31.090
for landmark legislation
affecting civil rights

00:54:31.090 --> 00:54:33.550
and economic development.

00:54:33.550 --> 00:54:35.110
During his tenure
in Congress, he

00:54:35.110 --> 00:54:38.920
served as chair of the
Joint Senate-House Economic

00:54:38.920 --> 00:54:42.220
Committee, and his other
committee assignments

00:54:42.220 --> 00:54:46.240
included the Ethics Committee,
Banking and Financial Services

00:54:46.240 --> 00:54:48.520
Committee, the
Committee on Education,

00:54:48.520 --> 00:54:51.130
and the Small
Business Committee.

00:54:51.130 --> 00:54:54.430
In 1996, he resigned
from Congress

00:54:54.430 --> 00:54:57.625
to assume the
presidency of the NAACP,

00:54:57.625 --> 00:55:00.730
where his leadership has
had a significant impact

00:55:00.730 --> 00:55:04.600
on a wide range of
civil rights issues.

00:55:04.600 --> 00:55:07.950
In addition to his
work with the NAACP,

00:55:07.950 --> 00:55:12.160
Mr. Mfume is active in the
world of higher education.

00:55:12.160 --> 00:55:16.180
He serves on the Morgan State
University Board of Regents,

00:55:16.180 --> 00:55:19.210
the Senior Advisory Committee
in the John F. Kennedy School

00:55:19.210 --> 00:55:22.050
of Government at
Harvard University,

00:55:22.050 --> 00:55:25.180
the Meyerhoff National Advisory
Board of the University

00:55:25.180 --> 00:55:28.630
of Maryland, and the board
of trustees of the Enterprise

00:55:28.630 --> 00:55:32.710
Foundation, and I believe
from our table conversation

00:55:32.710 --> 00:55:35.620
also on the Johns
Hopkins board now.

00:55:35.620 --> 00:55:38.890
He's a member of Big
Brothers and Big Sisters,

00:55:38.890 --> 00:55:41.350
and he is the author of
a critically acclaimed

00:55:41.350 --> 00:55:45.730
autobiography
entitled No Free Ride.

00:55:45.730 --> 00:55:48.640
When it comes to equal
opportunity and social progress

00:55:48.640 --> 00:55:52.870
for all Americans, we could
seek no stronger advocate.

00:55:52.870 --> 00:55:57.112
Please join me in welcoming
the honorable Kweisi Mfume.

00:55:57.112 --> 00:56:00.556
[APPLAUSE]

00:56:20.076 --> 00:56:21.200
MFUME: Thank you very much.

00:56:29.630 --> 00:56:33.230
Allow me, if I might,
to begin my remarks

00:56:33.230 --> 00:56:37.580
by thanking Dr.
Vest for those kind

00:56:37.580 --> 00:56:42.530
and overly gracious
set of comments,

00:56:42.530 --> 00:56:47.610
and for his leadership at
this historic university,

00:56:47.610 --> 00:56:50.150
but probably this morning,
more than anything else,

00:56:50.150 --> 00:56:55.730
for being a buffer between me
and the two very impressive

00:56:55.730 --> 00:56:59.570
students that spoke before me.

00:56:59.570 --> 00:57:03.680
I am particularly
happy, Dr. Vest,

00:57:03.680 --> 00:57:08.210
that you invoked the name
of Leon Higginbotham, who

00:57:08.210 --> 00:57:14.450
was a very dear
friend and probably

00:57:14.450 --> 00:57:19.190
the most important reason why
I left my work in the Congress

00:57:19.190 --> 00:57:21.830
to join the NAACP.

00:57:21.830 --> 00:57:26.510
After many conversations
with Judge Higginbotham

00:57:26.510 --> 00:57:31.640
and after many opportunities
to understand his reasoning as

00:57:31.640 --> 00:57:34.670
to why it was important
for me to do that,

00:57:34.670 --> 00:57:38.540
I ultimately did just
that and was here

00:57:38.540 --> 00:57:41.390
just a couple of months
ago to participate

00:57:41.390 --> 00:57:44.870
in his home-going
service and would

00:57:44.870 --> 00:57:49.610
say to particularly
students who may have not

00:57:49.610 --> 00:57:53.020
had the opportunity to
meet Leon Higginbotham

00:57:53.020 --> 00:57:55.910
that you find a way to
either get to the library

00:57:55.910 --> 00:57:58.120
or to get back on the
internet or to get

00:57:58.120 --> 00:58:02.480
to someone who did to find
out about this extraordinary

00:58:02.480 --> 00:58:04.520
individual.

00:58:04.520 --> 00:58:07.520
I'd also like to thank
all of the people who

00:58:07.520 --> 00:58:09.774
have made this morning's
event possible,

00:58:09.774 --> 00:58:10.940
and there are a lot of them.

00:58:10.940 --> 00:58:14.000
And I was particularly
happy last night

00:58:14.000 --> 00:58:18.360
that I got a chance
to meet many of them.

00:58:18.360 --> 00:58:22.070
I'd like, if I might, on a
point of personal privilege

00:58:22.070 --> 00:58:27.710
to really thank Dr. Leo Osgood
and Professor Michael Feld.

00:58:27.710 --> 00:58:32.840
They have, through perhaps
some difficulty but more

00:58:32.840 --> 00:58:37.130
importantly, through a number
of different circumstances

00:58:37.130 --> 00:58:39.240
that we probably
are not aware of,

00:58:39.240 --> 00:58:42.830
continued to find a way
to make this possible

00:58:42.830 --> 00:58:47.090
and to remind all of us
about the need to take time

00:58:47.090 --> 00:58:51.980
to commemorate and to remember.

00:58:51.980 --> 00:58:59.720
I want to also say to Provost
Robert Brown and all the others

00:58:59.720 --> 00:59:03.350
who have worked very hard on
this event that I do thank you,

00:59:03.350 --> 00:59:06.560
and I know I speak on behalf
of students who are here

00:59:06.560 --> 00:59:10.250
and those who are not
who recognize now,

00:59:10.250 --> 00:59:16.280
even if it is in hindsight, how
important this remembrance is.

00:59:16.280 --> 00:59:22.100
To Ms. Gomez and Mr. Pinkett,
I want to thank both of you

00:59:22.100 --> 00:59:24.350
for your energy,
for the opportunity

00:59:24.350 --> 00:59:28.310
to meet you last night, but
more importantly, for the energy

00:59:28.310 --> 00:59:31.070
and the sense of
understanding you displayed

00:59:31.070 --> 00:59:32.810
a little while ago
with respect to why

00:59:32.810 --> 00:59:35.920
it is important to remember.

00:59:35.920 --> 00:59:39.110
And I'd also, if I might,
like to acknowledge,

00:59:39.110 --> 00:59:41.370
as was the case
earlier, the presence

00:59:41.370 --> 00:59:45.320
of the mayor of Cambridge
and many, many other honored

00:59:45.320 --> 00:59:47.630
guests, not the
least of which are

00:59:47.630 --> 00:59:51.960
several distinguished members
of our organization, our state

00:59:51.960 --> 00:59:55.250
conference president here in the
New England area, Ms. Charlotte

00:59:55.250 --> 00:59:58.100
Nelson, who is
somewhere out there--

00:59:58.100 --> 01:00:04.010
Charlotte-- and the presidents
of our Boston and Cambridge

01:00:04.010 --> 01:00:07.100
chapters, Lennie
Alkins, Jackie Carol.

01:00:07.100 --> 01:00:09.540
I know [INAUDIBLE] is
here and a few others.

01:00:09.540 --> 01:00:13.280
Thank all of you for coming
out and supporting me

01:00:13.280 --> 01:00:17.240
and for coming out also to
work with this community

01:00:17.240 --> 01:00:18.770
as you do day in and day out.

01:00:18.770 --> 01:00:21.061
Could we give all of those
persons a round of applause?

01:00:21.061 --> 01:00:23.749
[APPLAUSE]

01:00:31.560 --> 01:00:33.150
I bring you greetings
this morning

01:00:33.150 --> 01:00:34.602
on behalf of the NAACP--

01:00:34.602 --> 01:00:38.560
our 1,700 branches
and 50 states,

01:00:38.560 --> 01:00:44.070
the District of Columbia,
Germany, Japan, and Korea.

01:00:44.070 --> 01:00:47.370
At the National Association
for the Advancement of Colored

01:00:47.370 --> 01:00:52.310
People, we believe that colored
people come in all colors,

01:00:52.310 --> 01:00:55.230
for it was the NAACP
that saw America

01:00:55.230 --> 01:00:57.120
through the troubled
years of Jim

01:00:57.120 --> 01:01:00.300
Crow and second-class
citizenship

01:01:00.300 --> 01:01:03.990
and legal lynchings, through
the years of manufactured

01:01:03.990 --> 01:01:08.040
grandfather clauses and poll
taxes and literacy tests where

01:01:08.040 --> 01:01:10.950
you had to tell how many
bubbles were in a bar of soap

01:01:10.950 --> 01:01:13.740
just to be allowed to vote.

01:01:13.740 --> 01:01:16.530
We fought over the
years the just fight

01:01:16.530 --> 01:01:21.270
to integrate the military and to
end official segregation as we

01:01:21.270 --> 01:01:25.290
knew it, and we found also a
way to help a nation divided

01:01:25.290 --> 01:01:29.760
against itself through the
confusion and the turbulence

01:01:29.760 --> 01:01:32.580
of the 1960s, and then
later, through the I-isms

01:01:32.580 --> 01:01:35.280
and the differences
of the 1980s.

01:01:35.280 --> 01:01:38.790
And so it is not for
us a matter of having

01:01:38.790 --> 01:01:42.480
come a long, long way,
but rather and instead, it

01:01:42.480 --> 01:01:47.280
is a matter of having still
yet a long, long way to go.

01:01:47.280 --> 01:01:52.050
That, in turn, begs the question
not when do we get there,

01:01:52.050 --> 01:01:55.560
but what path do we take?

01:01:55.560 --> 01:01:59.430
And that is where your themes
of teaching and learning

01:01:59.430 --> 01:02:03.360
and inclusion come into play.

01:02:03.360 --> 01:02:06.360
You see, the light really
does burn bright here

01:02:06.360 --> 01:02:10.200
at and for MIT.

01:02:10.200 --> 01:02:14.400
You are a place in many
respects where, for some people,

01:02:14.400 --> 01:02:17.550
dreams have come
true and given birth

01:02:17.550 --> 01:02:21.240
to other dreams of service
and academic excellence.

01:02:21.240 --> 01:02:24.600
You have been entrusted
with a mighty vision,

01:02:24.600 --> 01:02:29.520
and you have proven in many
respects to be worthy stewards.

01:02:29.520 --> 01:02:31.410
Some of you have worked
hard to make sure

01:02:31.410 --> 01:02:34.110
that your talents are
not buried in the ground,

01:02:34.110 --> 01:02:36.960
but rather invested
well in the arts

01:02:36.960 --> 01:02:43.470
and in the minds of our greatest
asset, our young people.

01:02:43.470 --> 01:02:48.120
The late Dr. Benjamin
Mays of Morehouse College

01:02:48.120 --> 01:02:52.170
once said that he or
she who starts behind

01:02:52.170 --> 01:02:57.900
in the race of life would
either have to run faster

01:02:57.900 --> 01:03:01.370
or forever remain behind.

01:03:01.370 --> 01:03:03.870
Young men and
women who have kept

01:03:03.870 --> 01:03:06.030
this tradition of
remembering alive now

01:03:06.030 --> 01:03:10.950
for all these 25 years,
in fact, have run faster.

01:03:10.950 --> 01:03:13.800
And so I applaud
them and those brave

01:03:13.800 --> 01:03:17.400
souls, no matter how small
in number they may be,

01:03:17.400 --> 01:03:20.070
who labor here at
this university, who,

01:03:20.070 --> 01:03:21.960
like tillers of
the soil, continue

01:03:21.960 --> 01:03:26.850
to push and to prod and cajole
for a greater university

01:03:26.850 --> 01:03:30.780
commitment to diversity
and equal opportunity.

01:03:30.780 --> 01:03:34.950
They know almost intuitively
that our ability as a nation

01:03:34.950 --> 01:03:37.140
to survive will
depend particularly

01:03:37.140 --> 01:03:40.140
on how we deal with
our increasingly

01:03:40.140 --> 01:03:42.330
pluralistic society.

01:03:42.330 --> 01:03:45.240
And so as a preface
to my remarks,

01:03:45.240 --> 01:03:47.820
I would challenge others
today to take the route

01:03:47.820 --> 01:03:51.420
that those individuals
have taken.

01:03:51.420 --> 01:03:54.090
It is one of embracing
diversity while seeking

01:03:54.090 --> 01:03:56.900
to promote reciprocal
understandings,

01:03:56.900 --> 01:04:00.400
recognizing as we do
that quite frankly,

01:04:00.400 --> 01:04:03.610
it's easier to do nothing.

01:04:03.610 --> 01:04:05.790
And so because we all
want to do something,

01:04:05.790 --> 01:04:10.320
let's start, if we might,
by putting in proper context

01:04:10.320 --> 01:04:16.800
the man who we have come to
commemorate this morning.

01:04:16.800 --> 01:04:20.940
The rabbi spoke of
the Old Testament.

01:04:20.940 --> 01:04:24.600
Let me go even beyond
the Book of Exodus

01:04:24.600 --> 01:04:28.590
to talk for just a moment
about the Book of Genesis,

01:04:28.590 --> 01:04:35.220
the 37th chapter, because it
underscores in many respects

01:04:35.220 --> 01:04:40.440
the age-old desire by a lot of
people to undercut visionaries

01:04:40.440 --> 01:04:45.520
by trying to do away
with their dreams.

01:04:45.520 --> 01:04:48.120
It says in the 37th
chapter that "and

01:04:48.120 --> 01:04:53.760
when they saw him from
afar, even before he

01:04:53.760 --> 01:04:57.690
had come near to
them, they conspired

01:04:57.690 --> 01:05:00.960
against him to slay him.

01:05:00.960 --> 01:05:08.340
And they said one to another,
behold, the dreamer cometh.

01:05:08.340 --> 01:05:12.000
Come now, therefore,
and let us slay him,

01:05:12.000 --> 01:05:15.240
and we will cast him
into some old pit,

01:05:15.240 --> 01:05:20.100
and we will say that some
evil beast has devoured him,

01:05:20.100 --> 01:05:26.310
and we shall see what
becomes of his dream."

01:05:26.310 --> 01:05:33.950
Martin Luther King, Jr,
un-awed by opinion, un-seduced

01:05:33.950 --> 01:05:39.020
by flattery,
undismayed by disaster,

01:05:39.020 --> 01:05:41.690
confronted life with the
courage of his convictions

01:05:41.690 --> 01:05:45.800
and confronted death with
the courage of his faith.

01:05:45.800 --> 01:05:52.880
And lest we ever forget, we are
talking about a young man, 26

01:05:52.880 --> 01:05:56.120
years of age when his
face first appeared

01:05:56.120 --> 01:06:00.710
on the cover of Time
magazine, 35 years of age

01:06:00.710 --> 01:06:05.170
when he won the Nobel Peace
Prize, 39 years of age

01:06:05.170 --> 01:06:08.720
on the afternoon of
his assassination.

01:06:08.720 --> 01:06:12.560
It is important to
understand that because it's

01:06:12.560 --> 01:06:19.100
important to have a full
appreciation of his capacity

01:06:19.100 --> 01:06:26.420
and his indignation over
the absence of justice.

01:06:26.420 --> 01:06:31.310
A Greek historian, at
least legend has it,

01:06:31.310 --> 01:06:38.900
was once asked when would
justice ever come to Athens.

01:06:38.900 --> 01:06:41.720
And after thinking about the
magnitude of the question,

01:06:41.720 --> 01:06:44.990
he thoughtfully replied
that justice would never

01:06:44.990 --> 01:06:50.660
come to Athens until all
those who were not injured

01:06:50.660 --> 01:06:56.210
were just as indignant
as all of those who are.

01:06:56.210 --> 01:06:59.570
And so I hope and
pray that those of you

01:06:59.570 --> 01:07:06.980
in this room this morning
who are Caucasian or white,

01:07:06.980 --> 01:07:11.810
that you understand the
indignity of those like myself

01:07:11.810 --> 01:07:15.860
who are not at the
scourge of racism

01:07:15.860 --> 01:07:18.730
and bigotry and
unequal treatment,

01:07:18.730 --> 01:07:24.920
and that you, in your own way,
will become just as indignant.

01:07:24.920 --> 01:07:28.970
I hope and pray that
those who are here today

01:07:28.970 --> 01:07:33.270
who are of Asian and
Indian, Hispanic,

01:07:33.270 --> 01:07:37.700
and Native American ancestry,
that you understand,

01:07:37.700 --> 01:07:41.660
as we must, the real
need never to give up

01:07:41.660 --> 01:07:44.720
on the idea of
coalition building,

01:07:44.720 --> 01:07:48.470
even when some in your
number and some in mine

01:07:48.470 --> 01:07:50.720
prefer to go the other
way and to talk only

01:07:50.720 --> 01:07:54.230
about our individual
agendas or the power

01:07:54.230 --> 01:07:57.080
of our individual groups.

01:07:57.080 --> 01:07:59.720
And I hope and pray
that those of you

01:07:59.720 --> 01:08:05.060
who sit here today who
are African-American

01:08:05.060 --> 01:08:12.050
or of African ancestry, that
you understand, as we must,

01:08:12.050 --> 01:08:19.370
the real need at some point
in time to get beyond blame,

01:08:19.370 --> 01:08:22.670
to get beyond excuses,
and to start once

01:08:22.670 --> 01:08:25.206
again doing for ourselves.

01:08:25.206 --> 01:08:28.182
[APPLAUSE]

01:08:34.630 --> 01:08:39.700
If we were to leave here
and to go over to the lab

01:08:39.700 --> 01:08:46.270
and sit before any computer
and to request from ourselves

01:08:46.270 --> 01:08:51.729
a simple computer print-out of
all the salient issues facing

01:08:51.729 --> 01:08:58.720
us as a nation, it is clear
that the list of problems

01:08:58.720 --> 01:09:01.330
to be printed out
by that computer

01:09:01.330 --> 01:09:05.140
would, in fact, be overwhelming.

01:09:05.140 --> 01:09:12.050
Institutionally, government,
military, church, and school

01:09:12.050 --> 01:09:17.470
are all under attack for either
real or imagined defects.

01:09:17.470 --> 01:09:20.500
Politically, the Joint
Center for Political Studies

01:09:20.500 --> 01:09:25.120
in Washington would remind us
that 35 years after passage

01:09:25.120 --> 01:09:30.430
of the Voting Rights Act and
30 years after outright efforts

01:09:30.430 --> 01:09:34.260
to register and to elect people
that still in this country,

01:09:34.260 --> 01:09:37.960
less than 7% of all
the elected officials

01:09:37.960 --> 01:09:42.790
are of African or Hispanic
or Asian ancestry.

01:09:42.790 --> 01:09:45.040
Socially, that same
print-out would

01:09:45.040 --> 01:09:49.180
suggest that the issue
of race and skin color

01:09:49.180 --> 01:09:53.979
still dominate too many aspects
of American life, both at home

01:09:53.979 --> 01:09:55.840
and abroad.

01:09:55.840 --> 01:10:00.550
Economically, it would suggest
to us in the clearest of terms

01:10:00.550 --> 01:10:02.560
that after four years
of Congressional

01:10:02.560 --> 01:10:06.160
acquiescence to the concept
of Robin Hood in reverse,

01:10:06.160 --> 01:10:10.060
the haves now have more, and
the have-nots have none at all.

01:10:10.060 --> 01:10:13.020
The gap continues to widen.

01:10:13.020 --> 01:10:16.450
[APPLAUSE]

01:10:18.880 --> 01:10:22.710
Educationally, it would
point out the obvious--

01:10:22.710 --> 01:10:24.490
that too many of
our public schools

01:10:24.490 --> 01:10:27.340
are overcrowded
and ill equipped,

01:10:27.340 --> 01:10:30.790
and drugs tend to be more
available than textbooks,

01:10:30.790 --> 01:10:33.160
and that too many young
people in those schools,

01:10:33.160 --> 01:10:37.570
because they are a lot there,
are being promoted because

01:10:37.570 --> 01:10:41.200
of their age or because
of their size, only

01:10:41.200 --> 01:10:43.540
to be rewarded at
the end of 12 years

01:10:43.540 --> 01:10:48.820
with the equivalent
document that would suggest

01:10:48.820 --> 01:10:50.350
it was a certificate
of attendance,

01:10:50.350 --> 01:10:54.940
but not a meaningful
high school diploma.

01:10:54.940 --> 01:10:59.500
And yet we know that the
student in those schools,

01:10:59.500 --> 01:11:02.800
not different from
students in other schools,

01:11:02.800 --> 01:11:05.200
have one thing
that defines them,

01:11:05.200 --> 01:11:08.560
and that is that the
student who makes the grade

01:11:08.560 --> 01:11:10.840
is still the one who
comes early and stays

01:11:10.840 --> 01:11:13.840
late to learn the
meaning of the lesson,

01:11:13.840 --> 01:11:17.920
but never to lessen the
meaning of the assignment,

01:11:17.920 --> 01:11:21.850
and that the teacher in those
schools who makes the grade

01:11:21.850 --> 01:11:24.940
is still the teacher who
teaches to touch a life

01:11:24.940 --> 01:11:28.980
and not just to make a living.

01:11:28.980 --> 01:11:32.370
Just a few months
from now, we will

01:11:32.370 --> 01:11:36.030
witness what many
in the press will

01:11:36.030 --> 01:11:42.270
refer to as a celebration of the
45th anniversary of the Supreme

01:11:42.270 --> 01:11:43.350
Court decision--

01:11:43.350 --> 01:11:49.260
Brown versus the Board of
Topeka, Kansas, when on May 17,

01:11:49.260 --> 01:11:58.290
1954, nine men robed in black
assembled on that historic day

01:11:58.290 --> 01:12:03.240
to announce their
unanimous verdict.

01:12:03.240 --> 01:12:07.920
There was, in fact,
dancing in the streets.

01:12:07.920 --> 01:12:10.380
At black colleges,
classes were suspended

01:12:10.380 --> 01:12:13.450
that day and parties
were hastily assembled,

01:12:13.450 --> 01:12:16.140
and there was dancing in the
streets of Richmond and Raleigh

01:12:16.140 --> 01:12:19.020
and Baltimore and
Washington, and people

01:12:19.020 --> 01:12:22.920
began to believe in their hearts
and in the inner-most parts

01:12:22.920 --> 01:12:27.600
of their being that our nation
was at long last launched

01:12:27.600 --> 01:12:31.590
on an unalterable course
with a firm determination

01:12:31.590 --> 01:12:34.950
that in terms of
public education,

01:12:34.950 --> 01:12:40.650
we were now prepared to overcome
the legacies of the past.

01:12:40.650 --> 01:12:46.780
But in 1969, just a
decade or so later,

01:12:46.780 --> 01:12:48.570
a high-ranking official
in the White House

01:12:48.570 --> 01:12:50.460
who is now a United
States senator

01:12:50.460 --> 01:12:52.320
advised the president
then in what

01:12:52.320 --> 01:12:55.650
later became known as the
celebrated "Memorandum

01:12:55.650 --> 01:12:58.560
on Public Education"
when he said,

01:12:58.560 --> 01:13:05.790
Mr. President, we have made
so much progress moving

01:13:05.790 --> 01:13:08.460
black people into the
mainstream of American

01:13:08.460 --> 01:13:12.600
economic, educational, and
social life that our nation's

01:13:12.600 --> 01:13:14.880
policies from this
point on with respect

01:13:14.880 --> 01:13:18.450
to the status of
those people ought

01:13:18.450 --> 01:13:22.860
be accorded benign neglect.

01:13:22.860 --> 01:13:28.050
That neglect once proposed as
benign in too many respects

01:13:28.050 --> 01:13:35.010
today for too many people is
a neglect that is malignant.

01:13:35.010 --> 01:13:39.820
And so when we think about
Dr. King and his capacity,

01:13:39.820 --> 01:13:42.360
remember also what Randall said.

01:13:42.360 --> 01:13:46.980
This was an ordinary
man who was called on

01:13:46.980 --> 01:13:50.230
to do extraordinary things.

01:13:50.230 --> 01:13:53.700
And I underscore that
because God still

01:13:53.700 --> 01:13:56.790
calls on ordinary people--

01:13:56.790 --> 01:14:00.150
all of us-- as
Booker T. Washington

01:14:00.150 --> 01:14:03.450
once said, to cast our
buckets down where we are,

01:14:03.450 --> 01:14:06.810
to pick our own battlefield,
and to make a difference

01:14:06.810 --> 01:14:09.350
in a real and meaningful way.

01:14:09.350 --> 01:14:13.620
When Dr. Vest talked
about these attacks

01:14:13.620 --> 01:14:16.560
on the ability of young
people to matriculate

01:14:16.560 --> 01:14:19.980
as a result of efforts
to deny opportunity,

01:14:19.980 --> 01:14:24.600
that's all tied into
the exact same thing.

01:14:24.600 --> 01:14:26.790
We know that
affirmative action has

01:14:26.790 --> 01:14:30.630
met with resistance
from its inception

01:14:30.630 --> 01:14:33.840
and that for the last
five years or so,

01:14:33.840 --> 01:14:36.440
there has been a
sustained attack

01:14:36.440 --> 01:14:39.480
nationwide, a movement
throughout the country

01:14:39.480 --> 01:14:44.130
to gut and to destroy
affirmative action as we know

01:14:44.130 --> 01:14:48.810
it, and in the process, destroy
diversity programs as well,

01:14:48.810 --> 01:14:54.690
programs both in the workplace
and on college campuses.

01:14:54.690 --> 01:15:01.320
Prop 209, Initiative 200,
the University of Michigan

01:15:01.320 --> 01:15:04.410
case which is now pending,
the Hopwood decision--

01:15:04.410 --> 01:15:08.070
in the aggregate, they
chip away at the ability

01:15:08.070 --> 01:15:10.800
of people who come behind
you, or at least want

01:15:10.800 --> 01:15:15.360
to come behind you, students,
to sit where you sit.

01:15:15.360 --> 01:15:21.510
Now, interestingly enough,
the rationale for this attack

01:15:21.510 --> 01:15:27.000
has been unsubstantiated reports
of widespread colorblindness.

01:15:27.000 --> 01:15:29.530
People just say, well, you
know, things are different

01:15:29.530 --> 01:15:32.430
and were a colorblind
society, and so we

01:15:32.430 --> 01:15:37.920
don't need to help you
with a Hispanic surname.

01:15:37.920 --> 01:15:41.460
We don't need to help
you because you happen

01:15:41.460 --> 01:15:43.830
to be of African ancestry.

01:15:43.830 --> 01:15:46.110
We don't really need
to help you, either,

01:15:46.110 --> 01:15:48.960
because you come
from the Pacific Rim.

01:15:48.960 --> 01:15:53.280
We don't have to help you
who grew up in Appalachia.

01:15:53.280 --> 01:15:55.650
We don't have to do
anything for anybody

01:15:55.650 --> 01:15:58.530
because colorblindness
has broken out

01:15:58.530 --> 01:16:02.040
throughout this society.

01:16:02.040 --> 01:16:06.150
Oh, if that were the
case, this program

01:16:06.150 --> 01:16:08.790
would be more than
a celebration.

01:16:08.790 --> 01:16:13.080
It would be a
magnificent celebration.

01:16:13.080 --> 01:16:16.500
And so I say to you
and remind myself,

01:16:16.500 --> 01:16:22.770
as we think about why
we have come together,

01:16:22.770 --> 01:16:25.680
what it means to
remember Dr. King

01:16:25.680 --> 01:16:31.230
and what our own personal
challenge is, that it is not

01:16:31.230 --> 01:16:37.050
so much the hypocrisy
of the past, the things

01:16:37.050 --> 01:16:39.960
that we know about that
we find repulsive--

01:16:39.960 --> 01:16:44.790
the institution of slavery, the
attacks on individuals because

01:16:44.790 --> 01:16:48.630
of their religious beliefs, the
attacks on individuals because

01:16:48.630 --> 01:16:52.200
of their surnames or because
of their sexual preferences.

01:16:52.200 --> 01:16:58.140
It is not so much the
hypocrisies of a past

01:16:58.140 --> 01:17:01.710
as much as it is for
those people of color

01:17:01.710 --> 01:17:04.880
the hypocrisies of the present.

01:17:04.880 --> 01:17:09.020
That's what should concern us.

01:17:09.020 --> 01:17:13.580
Item number one.

01:17:13.580 --> 01:17:17.510
In 1990, the Urban Institute--

01:17:17.510 --> 01:17:22.460
bipartisan funding,
bipartisan participation--

01:17:22.460 --> 01:17:25.250
conducted a nationwide
study over a number of years

01:17:25.250 --> 01:17:29.810
and concluded unequivocally
that there was unequal treatment

01:17:29.810 --> 01:17:34.100
of minority job seekers.

01:17:34.100 --> 01:17:35.120
Item number two.

01:17:35.120 --> 01:17:38.030
In 1991, the Holiday
Health Spa Club chain

01:17:38.030 --> 01:17:41.570
was found to have
systematically discriminated

01:17:41.570 --> 01:17:44.930
against women of color.

01:17:44.930 --> 01:17:46.370
Item number three.

01:17:46.370 --> 01:17:51.590
In 1993, Denny's
Restaurant settled

01:17:51.590 --> 01:17:53.600
claims of discrimination
because they

01:17:53.600 --> 01:17:56.210
refused to serve
black customers who

01:17:56.210 --> 01:17:58.850
happened to be six
gentlemen guarding

01:17:58.850 --> 01:18:01.190
the president as Secret
Service agents prepared

01:18:01.190 --> 01:18:05.930
to take a bullet that day
to preserve democracy.

01:18:05.930 --> 01:18:09.890
Item number four, 1994.

01:18:09.890 --> 01:18:15.140
The Chevy Chase Bank agrees
to an $11 million settlement

01:18:15.140 --> 01:18:21.500
because they red-lined
communities and neighborhoods,

01:18:21.500 --> 01:18:24.020
or at least were
accused of doing it,

01:18:24.020 --> 01:18:27.170
and refused to go into
court to defend themselves,

01:18:27.170 --> 01:18:30.230
as many other institutions
had previously done.

01:18:32.880 --> 01:18:36.660
Item 5, 1995.

01:18:36.660 --> 01:18:38.940
The Glass Ceiling
Commission concludes

01:18:38.940 --> 01:18:43.710
that women, Hispanics, and
African-Americans are still

01:18:43.710 --> 01:18:48.480
disproportionately represented
among the nation's working

01:18:48.480 --> 01:18:50.940
poor.

01:18:50.940 --> 01:18:54.930
Item 6, 1996.

01:18:54.930 --> 01:18:59.790
Texaco, the infamous
tape where executives

01:18:59.790 --> 01:19:04.230
are caught loosely making
fun of Jewish holidays

01:19:04.230 --> 01:19:06.810
and black holidays and
referring to people

01:19:06.810 --> 01:19:12.210
as jelly beans of color
and talking very, very

01:19:12.210 --> 01:19:17.160
openly about why it's important
not to let those people

01:19:17.160 --> 01:19:19.680
have an opportunity.

01:19:19.680 --> 01:19:24.240
And lastly, 1998.

01:19:24.240 --> 01:19:27.090
Data released under the
Home Mortgage Disclosure Act

01:19:27.090 --> 01:19:31.200
showed nationwide that
black and Latino borrowers

01:19:31.200 --> 01:19:34.560
were turned down at
increasingly higher rates

01:19:34.560 --> 01:19:36.840
when applying for
mortgage loans.

01:19:36.840 --> 01:19:41.280
More than 50% of all
those who applied

01:19:41.280 --> 01:19:45.130
were denied loans at
banks across this nation.

01:19:45.130 --> 01:19:48.540
And so, regrettably,
discrimination

01:19:48.540 --> 01:19:51.810
is not an article of the past.

01:19:51.810 --> 01:19:56.470
It is instead an
article of the present.

01:19:56.470 --> 01:19:59.520
And so what then
really becomes our role

01:19:59.520 --> 01:20:04.710
in dealing with what
we are faced with?

01:20:04.710 --> 01:20:07.890
We find ourselves
living at a time when

01:20:07.890 --> 01:20:10.870
our sense of community can no
longer be taken for granted.

01:20:10.870 --> 01:20:14.300
That's for sure.

01:20:14.300 --> 01:20:17.780
We find ourselves at a time
where information technology

01:20:17.780 --> 01:20:21.770
has made it possible for us
to communicate across oceans,

01:20:21.770 --> 01:20:25.850
across continents, across every
conceivable boundary of race

01:20:25.850 --> 01:20:28.880
and culture, and to do that
almost instantaneously.

01:20:28.880 --> 01:20:32.450
But our technology
has almost also

01:20:32.450 --> 01:20:35.960
made it possible for us to
be able to live and work

01:20:35.960 --> 01:20:41.660
in complete isolation from our
neighbors and fellow citizens.

01:20:41.660 --> 01:20:43.910
Things have changed.

01:20:43.910 --> 01:20:45.440
It'd be nice to be
like these birds.

01:20:45.440 --> 01:20:47.730
Just fly around all day long.

01:20:47.730 --> 01:20:48.230
You know?

01:20:48.230 --> 01:20:50.600
[CHUCKLING]

01:20:50.600 --> 01:20:52.280
Have a little bite
to eat on that table

01:20:52.280 --> 01:20:56.940
and fly up to the balcony.

01:20:56.940 --> 01:20:58.430
But things have changed.

01:20:58.430 --> 01:21:01.220
[CHUCKLING]

01:21:01.220 --> 01:21:05.060
The common experiences that have
made us recognize each other

01:21:05.060 --> 01:21:07.820
as members of a
community of Americans

01:21:07.820 --> 01:21:10.700
are becoming less
common each year.

01:21:10.700 --> 01:21:17.270
Scab labor, unbridled poverty,
second-class citizenship,

01:21:17.270 --> 01:21:20.330
and violent crime chip
away at that sense

01:21:20.330 --> 01:21:22.910
of community every day.

01:21:22.910 --> 01:21:26.510
Hate speech, hate
groups, hate radio,

01:21:26.510 --> 01:21:29.090
and hate crimes are
attempting to divide

01:21:29.090 --> 01:21:32.900
those same communities
like never before,

01:21:32.900 --> 01:21:37.380
and yet we know if we lose
that sense of community,

01:21:37.380 --> 01:21:40.100
that same community that
Martin King spoke about

01:21:40.100 --> 01:21:42.440
from an old Birmingham
jail, we lose

01:21:42.440 --> 01:21:46.550
much of what has made America
distinctive among the nations

01:21:46.550 --> 01:21:48.320
of the world.

01:21:48.320 --> 01:21:50.840
As foreign visitors
have observed

01:21:50.840 --> 01:21:53.900
since the beginning
of this republic,

01:21:53.900 --> 01:21:56.480
America's greatest strength
has been her identity

01:21:56.480 --> 01:22:00.650
as a group and a collection
of different people

01:22:00.650 --> 01:22:03.680
whose common destiny
was more powerful

01:22:03.680 --> 01:22:08.280
than their diverse backgrounds
or stations in life.

01:22:08.280 --> 01:22:11.090
And that is why people
in this country,

01:22:11.090 --> 01:22:15.830
under the umbrella of the NAACP
and hundreds of other groups,

01:22:15.830 --> 01:22:17.930
have always made
an attempt to try

01:22:17.930 --> 01:22:23.260
to communicate across lines of
race, class, age, and religion.

01:22:23.260 --> 01:22:27.540
It's not because they
were foolish or foolhardy.

01:22:27.540 --> 01:22:32.780
It's because they understood
the fact that it is harder

01:22:32.780 --> 01:22:37.280
to accept that the
road less traveled

01:22:37.280 --> 01:22:41.270
is the road less certain, but
it is the proper road for us

01:22:41.270 --> 01:22:42.170
to be on anyway.

01:22:42.170 --> 01:22:45.260
It is that path that
Randall talked about

01:22:45.260 --> 01:22:47.239
that we beat out of
the wilderness that

01:22:47.239 --> 01:22:48.030
makes a difference.

01:22:48.030 --> 01:22:51.770
The really, really
understanding of what

01:22:51.770 --> 01:22:54.800
it means to be on a road less
traveled, where there are not

01:22:54.800 --> 01:22:57.920
street lights and
paved sidewalks,

01:22:57.920 --> 01:23:01.580
where there is at each turn
some degree of uncertainty,

01:23:01.580 --> 01:23:05.810
but certainly at the end of
the road, if we are persistent,

01:23:05.810 --> 01:23:09.200
a great deal of satisfaction.

01:23:09.200 --> 01:23:14.710
That opposite path, the one
that's paved and well lit,

01:23:14.710 --> 01:23:18.360
the one that we
think has no curves--

01:23:18.360 --> 01:23:23.700
that's a path of cynicism,
contempt, distrust,

01:23:23.700 --> 01:23:24.900
and suspicion.

01:23:24.900 --> 01:23:30.520
It is best espoused by the
Timothy McVays of this world.

01:23:30.520 --> 01:23:36.390
And that road, to be sure,
leads to separatism, suspicion,

01:23:36.390 --> 01:23:39.120
division, and destruction.

01:23:39.120 --> 01:23:42.240
And so collectively
and individually,

01:23:42.240 --> 01:23:45.270
our charge has been
renewed, regrettably,

01:23:45.270 --> 01:23:50.550
by an old plague that
has come back to America,

01:23:50.550 --> 01:23:53.850
a plague that has resurfaced
with great abandon,

01:23:53.850 --> 01:23:58.260
a national scourge, if
you will, of insensitivity

01:23:58.260 --> 01:24:03.270
and intolerance, whether it is
the repugnant act of burning

01:24:03.270 --> 01:24:06.060
black churches or
desecrating synagogues,

01:24:06.060 --> 01:24:09.750
whether it is increased violence
from militia groups or bombings

01:24:09.750 --> 01:24:12.750
of federal buildings or
demonstrations against

01:24:12.750 --> 01:24:17.790
immigrants simply because
they cannot speak as we do.

01:24:17.790 --> 01:24:25.620
Tolerance, for too many, has
once again become a dirty word.

01:24:25.620 --> 01:24:30.090
You look at what happened out
there in Laramie, Wyoming.

01:24:30.090 --> 01:24:34.170
The student matriculating--
and because someone thought

01:24:34.170 --> 01:24:37.320
they should determine what
his sexual preference ought

01:24:37.320 --> 01:24:39.920
to be decided that
they and not God

01:24:39.920 --> 01:24:43.050
had the right to take his life.

01:24:43.050 --> 01:24:46.360
Look what happened in
June in Jasper, Texas.

01:24:46.360 --> 01:24:50.820
300 miles from
nowhere, James Byrd Jr,

01:24:50.820 --> 01:24:53.490
on a street corner
trying to get home,

01:24:53.490 --> 01:24:57.360
gets grabbed and drug by
a truck for three miles

01:24:57.360 --> 01:25:00.390
until his arms
and neck and limbs

01:25:00.390 --> 01:25:05.940
are dismembered because he
just happened to be black.

01:25:05.940 --> 01:25:09.280
No, if Dr. King were
here, he'd remind us

01:25:09.280 --> 01:25:15.278
that Jim Crow Sr. is dead, but
Jim Crow Jr. is alive and well.

01:25:15.278 --> 01:25:18.764
[APPLAUSE]

01:25:24.250 --> 01:25:31.670
And so the great moral
challenge for all of us

01:25:31.670 --> 01:25:36.380
is to separate the
truth from the trick,

01:25:36.380 --> 01:25:40.700
and the challenge
rests on our shoulders

01:25:40.700 --> 01:25:45.680
because in an era of smaller
vision, rampant apathy,

01:25:45.680 --> 01:25:49.580
and celebrated mediocrity,
we so desperately

01:25:49.580 --> 01:25:53.540
need those men and women who
will stand up and speak out

01:25:53.540 --> 01:25:55.700
for that which is
right and to fight back

01:25:55.700 --> 01:25:59.750
against that which is
wrong, to really mean it

01:25:59.750 --> 01:26:05.165
when we say that racism, sexism,
and anti-Semitism are wrong,

01:26:05.165 --> 01:26:07.280
to know as a matter
of critical fact

01:26:07.280 --> 01:26:09.680
that black bigotry is
just as cruel and evil

01:26:09.680 --> 01:26:14.120
as white bigotry, to
understand intuitively

01:26:14.120 --> 01:26:17.870
that xenophobia and homophobia
and immigrant-bashing and

01:26:17.870 --> 01:26:21.680
union-bashing and city-bashing
deplete us as a nation.

01:26:21.680 --> 01:26:24.980
They rob us of some
lofty place in history

01:26:24.980 --> 01:26:28.190
and relegate us back
to where we have been

01:26:28.190 --> 01:26:32.690
and regrettably, in many
respects, are still now.

01:26:32.690 --> 01:26:36.860
And so in speaking out as
Dr. King would speak out,

01:26:36.860 --> 01:26:42.330
we must be honest and true
to our own sense of fairness,

01:26:42.330 --> 01:26:46.430
for the great enemy of the
truth is very often not the lie.

01:26:46.430 --> 01:26:48.200
That's deliberate.

01:26:48.200 --> 01:26:49.070
It's contrived.

01:26:49.070 --> 01:26:51.110
It's dishonest.

01:26:51.110 --> 01:26:53.930
The great enemy of
the truth very often

01:26:53.930 --> 01:27:00.200
is the myth because that is
persistent and persuasive and

01:27:00.200 --> 01:27:03.350
unrealistic.

01:27:03.350 --> 01:27:08.690
Too often, we hold fast to the
conclusions of other people.

01:27:08.690 --> 01:27:11.720
We subject all facts to a
kind of pre-fabricated set

01:27:11.720 --> 01:27:12.750
of interpretations.

01:27:12.750 --> 01:27:17.360
As my grandmother said, we
enjoy the comfort of opinion

01:27:17.360 --> 01:27:20.060
without the
discomfort of thought.

01:27:20.060 --> 01:27:24.410
And yet we have spent 5,000
years as a race of human beings

01:27:24.410 --> 01:27:28.970
trying to drag ourselves
out of the primeval slime

01:27:28.970 --> 01:27:32.830
by searching for truths
and moral absolutes,

01:27:32.830 --> 01:27:35.840
and yet in its purest
form, truth is not

01:27:35.840 --> 01:27:38.210
a polite tap on the shoulder.

01:27:38.210 --> 01:27:40.520
It is, as Martin Luther
King reminded us,

01:27:40.520 --> 01:27:42.205
a howling reproach.

01:27:42.205 --> 01:27:46.370
And what Moses brought down
from Mount Sinai 2,000 years ago

01:27:46.370 --> 01:27:50.828
was not the 10 suggestions, but
rather a blueprint for life.

01:27:50.828 --> 01:27:54.314
[APPLAUSE]

01:28:00.290 --> 01:28:02.120
Marabel Gomez said
it earlier, and I

01:28:02.120 --> 01:28:06.440
think it bears repeating as we
talk and remember and reflect

01:28:06.440 --> 01:28:11.130
on the life of Dr. King, those
words that said that we--

01:28:11.130 --> 01:28:15.110
we-- are confronted with
the fierce urgency of now

01:28:15.110 --> 01:28:18.740
and that in this unfolding
conundrum of life and history,

01:28:18.740 --> 01:28:22.250
there really is such a
thing as being too late,

01:28:22.250 --> 01:28:27.200
for procrastination is still
the great thief of time.

01:28:27.200 --> 01:28:29.690
And so consequently,
students, the degree

01:28:29.690 --> 01:28:33.530
that you will one
day receive from MIT

01:28:33.530 --> 01:28:38.150
will, in fact,
represent many things.

01:28:38.150 --> 01:28:43.940
It will be a reward for
your academic excellence.

01:28:43.940 --> 01:28:46.610
It will be a reminder of
a debt that you can never

01:28:46.610 --> 01:28:48.434
repay your parents.

01:28:48.434 --> 01:28:50.210
[CHUCKLING]

01:28:50.210 --> 01:28:53.840
It will be both a source
of relief and respect

01:28:53.840 --> 01:28:55.950
to your professors.

01:28:55.950 --> 01:29:02.060
But more than anything, it
will be a license to learn.

01:29:02.060 --> 01:29:05.940
And so age has given me the
arrogance and experience

01:29:05.940 --> 01:29:09.390
has given me the urgency
to tell you what life looks

01:29:09.390 --> 01:29:12.660
like from my side of the river.

01:29:12.660 --> 01:29:15.510
My generation was the first
to think that we might not

01:29:15.510 --> 01:29:20.160
have any time at all,
and your generation

01:29:20.160 --> 01:29:23.112
is the first to be
born knowing it.

01:29:23.112 --> 01:29:26.280
And so there will be
those beyond these doors

01:29:26.280 --> 01:29:30.330
and beyond this
activity today who

01:29:30.330 --> 01:29:35.220
will counsel you to be silent
in this reactionary time.

01:29:35.220 --> 01:29:39.090
They will suggest, students,
that you look the other way

01:29:39.090 --> 01:29:41.430
and hope for the best.

01:29:41.430 --> 01:29:44.970
But I refuse to stand mute
when opportunity is denied

01:29:44.970 --> 01:29:48.030
and justice is deferred,
and I challenge you not

01:29:48.030 --> 01:29:50.660
to stand mute also.

01:29:50.660 --> 01:29:52.770
And so when the timid
come running to you

01:29:52.770 --> 01:29:56.670
to say that they fear
even to try anymore,

01:29:56.670 --> 01:29:59.820
we must reply as Martin Luther
King did from an old Birmingham

01:29:59.820 --> 01:30:03.090
jail that now is the time.

01:30:03.090 --> 01:30:06.750
When you are told to wait for
tomorrow or the next tomorrow,

01:30:06.750 --> 01:30:09.790
for the next election
or the next generation,

01:30:09.790 --> 01:30:13.560
we must reply that
now is the time.

01:30:13.560 --> 01:30:18.380
I believe and I humbly
submit to you this morning

01:30:18.380 --> 01:30:23.820
that we must use this
occasion, this glittering

01:30:23.820 --> 01:30:28.050
reminder of the success
of our experience,

01:30:28.050 --> 01:30:32.010
to recommit ourselves to
sharing a basic dream.

01:30:32.010 --> 01:30:35.780
It is the dream of Martin Luther
King and Fannie Lou Hamer,

01:30:35.780 --> 01:30:37.820
the dream of Dubois
and Washington

01:30:37.820 --> 01:30:40.620
and Tubman and Douglass,
the dream of all

01:30:40.620 --> 01:30:42.630
those nameless and
faceless people

01:30:42.630 --> 01:30:46.050
who made their bodies
bridges over generations

01:30:46.050 --> 01:30:51.060
that you might run across and
one day get to the university.

01:30:51.060 --> 01:30:55.500
And we must do that not just
through our prose, our poetry,

01:30:55.500 --> 01:31:00.650
or our prayers, but also
through our actions--

01:31:00.650 --> 01:31:05.770
action which removes a
large part of our distress

01:31:05.770 --> 01:31:10.780
by changing the conditions
around us that created it.

01:31:10.780 --> 01:31:14.330
And so as I go to my
seat, let me say to you,

01:31:14.330 --> 01:31:18.530
and again remind
myself, that I have not

01:31:18.530 --> 01:31:25.320
given up on the American idea
or the American possibility.

01:31:25.320 --> 01:31:30.300
And I, like Dr. King, would
urge you not to give up also.

01:31:30.300 --> 01:31:32.370
I am convinced that
this nation still

01:31:32.370 --> 01:31:36.630
stands before the world as
perhaps the last expression

01:31:36.630 --> 01:31:42.270
of a possibility of mankind
devising a social order where

01:31:42.270 --> 01:31:44.670
justice is the
supreme ruler and law

01:31:44.670 --> 01:31:48.780
is but its instrument, where
freedom is the dominant creed

01:31:48.780 --> 01:31:51.780
and order but its
principle, where equity

01:31:51.780 --> 01:31:54.480
is the common practice
and fraternity

01:31:54.480 --> 01:32:00.270
the true human condition,
and to take that belief

01:32:00.270 --> 01:32:06.000
and to run with it beyond this
university, beyond your years

01:32:06.000 --> 01:32:09.840
of youth, and beyond
all else, and to make

01:32:09.840 --> 01:32:16.200
a real difference in this
nation and in your generation.

01:32:16.200 --> 01:32:23.920
And I challenge you, as Dr. King
would, to do that right now.

01:32:23.920 --> 01:32:29.350
And when they saw him
from afar, even before he

01:32:29.350 --> 01:32:34.610
had come near to them,
they conspired against him

01:32:34.610 --> 01:32:36.760
to slay him.

01:32:36.760 --> 01:32:44.281
And they said one to another,
behold, the dreamer cometh.

01:32:44.281 --> 01:32:46.760
Come now, therefore,
and let us slay him.

01:32:46.760 --> 01:32:50.230
We will cast him
into some old pit

01:32:50.230 --> 01:32:53.680
and say that some evil
beast has devoured him,

01:32:53.680 --> 01:32:58.930
and we shall see what
becomes of his dream.

01:32:58.930 --> 01:32:59.786
Thank you.

01:32:59.786 --> 01:33:01.650
[APPLAUSE]

01:33:01.650 --> 01:33:04.530
SMITH: Thank you again,
the honorable Kweisi Mfume,

01:33:04.530 --> 01:33:08.190
for his inspiring words.

01:33:08.190 --> 01:33:10.050
We really appreciate
listening to them,

01:33:10.050 --> 01:33:13.470
and I know as a student, I
can take a lot of that back

01:33:13.470 --> 01:33:15.300
and use it in my future life.

01:33:15.300 --> 01:33:16.549
So thank you again.

01:33:16.549 --> 01:33:18.046
Please [INAUDIBLE].

01:33:18.046 --> 01:33:21.539
[APPLAUSE]

01:33:26.040 --> 01:33:29.160
I now have the honor of
introducing Chancellor Lawrence

01:33:29.160 --> 01:33:34.380
S. Bachau, who will present
the 1999 MLK Leadership Awards.

01:33:34.380 --> 01:33:35.130
Chancellor Bachau.

01:33:38.595 --> 01:33:39.534
[APPLAUSE]

01:33:39.534 --> 01:33:40.575
BACHAU: Thank you, Shana.

01:33:45.030 --> 01:33:46.950
Thank you very much, Shana.

01:33:46.950 --> 01:33:50.070
First, I would like to thank
the committee for the privilege

01:33:50.070 --> 01:33:52.170
and honor of being
able to be here

01:33:52.170 --> 01:33:54.780
today to present these awards.

01:33:54.780 --> 01:33:59.120
This is a day in which we
not only remember Dr. King,

01:33:59.120 --> 01:34:02.880
remember his teachings,
remember his legacy,

01:34:02.880 --> 01:34:05.160
but we also honor
him by recognizing

01:34:05.160 --> 01:34:07.980
those members of
our community who

01:34:07.980 --> 01:34:10.170
through their actions
and their deeds

01:34:10.170 --> 01:34:13.830
have really embraced and
personified his ideals, who

01:34:13.830 --> 01:34:18.030
have, in Randall's words, found
their own way, their path,

01:34:18.030 --> 01:34:20.370
who've created their own road.

01:34:20.370 --> 01:34:23.430
In doing so, we recognize
a member of our faculty,

01:34:23.430 --> 01:34:27.510
we recognize one of our
alumni, and we recognize

01:34:27.510 --> 01:34:29.940
one of our students among us.

01:34:29.940 --> 01:34:32.760
In many ways, I think
this is a fitting tribute

01:34:32.760 --> 01:34:36.090
that we look across MIT to
the various generations that

01:34:36.090 --> 01:34:40.140
are represented
within our community.

01:34:40.140 --> 01:34:44.220
Before I would like to call up
our honorees today, however,

01:34:44.220 --> 01:34:48.630
I have to say something not
as chancellor, but rather

01:34:48.630 --> 01:34:52.050
as a member of our faculty.

01:34:52.050 --> 01:34:55.080
And I really direct my
comments largely in response

01:34:55.080 --> 01:35:00.510
to, I think, the remarkable
words of Randall,

01:35:00.510 --> 01:35:04.020
the remarkable words of Maribel.

01:35:04.020 --> 01:35:06.390
Those of us who have the
privilege of teaching here

01:35:06.390 --> 01:35:13.260
at MIT really bear an
extraordinary responsibility

01:35:13.260 --> 01:35:17.730
because we are entrusted
with absolutely extraordinary

01:35:17.730 --> 01:35:19.390
students.

01:35:19.390 --> 01:35:23.460
And you see today before you not
only in our student speakers,

01:35:23.460 --> 01:35:26.080
but also in our students
and our alumni who

01:35:26.080 --> 01:35:30.060
we are about to honor the
great privilege that we have,

01:35:30.060 --> 01:35:33.780
and indeed, the great burden
and responsibility that we

01:35:33.780 --> 01:35:40.620
take among ourselves to try and
lead, to guide, and to inspire.

01:35:40.620 --> 01:35:44.850
There are times in which I
think that as faculty at MIT,

01:35:44.850 --> 01:35:48.060
we should be required to
take the Hippocratic oath.

01:35:48.060 --> 01:35:50.770
First, do no harm.

01:35:50.770 --> 01:35:53.670
We really have such
remarkable students,

01:35:53.670 --> 01:35:56.940
and they constantly remind
us what a truly remarkable

01:35:56.940 --> 01:35:57.810
place this is.

01:35:57.810 --> 01:36:00.564
Randall, Mirabel, thank you
again for your inspiring words.

01:36:00.564 --> 01:36:03.910
[APPLAUSE]

01:36:08.220 --> 01:36:11.600
Our first honoree today is
a member of our faculty--

01:36:11.600 --> 01:36:12.420
Alex Slocum.

01:36:12.420 --> 01:36:15.610
Alex, would you
please come up here?

01:36:15.610 --> 01:36:18.956
[APPLAUSE]

01:36:22.790 --> 01:36:26.360
Alex is a remarkable person
in many different dimensions.

01:36:26.360 --> 01:36:31.980
He happens to be one of our
most gifted teachers at MIT.

01:36:31.980 --> 01:36:36.950
He happens to be
one of our most--

01:36:36.950 --> 01:36:39.830
and I mean this warmly, Alex--

01:36:39.830 --> 01:36:43.040
unusual personalities and
distinctive personalities

01:36:43.040 --> 01:36:44.880
at MIT.

01:36:44.880 --> 01:36:47.990
And I think Alex
embraces the very

01:36:47.990 --> 01:36:53.270
best of what we hope an MIT
faculty member would be--

01:36:53.270 --> 01:36:58.700
a scholar of the first
rank, an inspiring teacher,

01:36:58.700 --> 01:37:03.740
and somebody who also pays
extraordinary attention

01:37:03.740 --> 01:37:05.100
to his students.

01:37:05.100 --> 01:37:09.800
I would like to read to you from
the letter from Dr. Vest that

01:37:09.800 --> 01:37:13.520
recognizes Alex for
his accomplishments.

01:37:13.520 --> 01:37:16.790
In his letter of nomination,
Professor [INAUDIBLE]----

01:37:16.790 --> 01:37:20.510
this is Alex's department
head-- mechanical engineering--

01:37:20.510 --> 01:37:23.240
cited your innovative and
intellectually captivating

01:37:23.240 --> 01:37:25.760
efforts with MIT
minority students,

01:37:25.760 --> 01:37:27.980
and particularly your work
on activities sponsored

01:37:27.980 --> 01:37:31.700
by the Urban Design Corps in
the engineering design workshop

01:37:31.700 --> 01:37:34.340
of the second summer program.

01:37:34.340 --> 01:37:37.490
Creating and developing
products under your supervision

01:37:37.490 --> 01:37:39.350
has been an
invaluable experience

01:37:39.350 --> 01:37:42.410
for many young people
from diverse backgrounds.

01:37:42.410 --> 01:37:46.190
As an enthusiastic
teacher and caring mentor,

01:37:46.190 --> 01:37:49.370
you have helped these aspiring
engineers and designers

01:37:49.370 --> 01:37:51.950
to acquire the knowledge
and skills that

01:37:51.950 --> 01:37:54.680
lead not only to
greater self-confidence,

01:37:54.680 --> 01:37:58.340
but also to academic and
professional success.

01:37:58.340 --> 01:38:01.460
These generous and
lasting contributions

01:38:01.460 --> 01:38:03.740
to the lives of
your students embody

01:38:03.740 --> 01:38:07.310
the essence of Dr. King's
philosophy and vision.

01:38:07.310 --> 01:38:10.400
Alex, it's my privilege
to recognize you today

01:38:10.400 --> 01:38:13.706
as one of the winners of the
Martin Luther King Achievement

01:38:13.706 --> 01:38:14.206
Award.

01:38:14.206 --> 01:38:15.192
Congratulations.

01:38:15.192 --> 01:38:18.643
[APPLAUSE]

01:38:21.879 --> 01:38:22.587
SLOCUM: Oh, cool.

01:38:27.987 --> 01:38:28.570
Thanks, Larry.

01:38:28.570 --> 01:38:31.480
I appreciate it.

01:38:31.480 --> 01:38:35.800
I wrote a little poem
inspired by Mr. Mfume

01:38:35.800 --> 01:38:38.390
because I usually do things
kind of at the moment,

01:38:38.390 --> 01:38:41.310
take what I have, and try
to build a road with it.

01:38:41.310 --> 01:38:44.090
So let me try this.

01:38:44.090 --> 01:38:48.800
To win a battle, the pen
is mightier than the sword.

01:38:48.800 --> 01:38:51.710
To build a road, we
will find that the bite

01:38:51.710 --> 01:38:55.160
is mightier than the bulldozer.

01:38:55.160 --> 01:38:58.520
Two extraordinary students
started me on my road--

01:38:58.520 --> 01:39:00.915
Mark Graham, who's
over here somewhere--

01:39:00.915 --> 01:39:02.720
Mark, stand up for
a minute, please--

01:39:02.720 --> 01:39:04.760
and Marty Culpepper,
who I think Mr. Mfume

01:39:04.760 --> 01:39:07.620
met at dinner last night.

01:39:07.620 --> 01:39:10.070
I was just your average,
ordinary geek patenting away,

01:39:10.070 --> 01:39:11.150
having fun.

01:39:11.150 --> 01:39:13.940
And these two young
people made me

01:39:13.940 --> 01:39:16.430
realize that there's
a big wilderness,

01:39:16.430 --> 01:39:19.820
and if we build this road,
everybody can be a lot happier.

01:39:22.770 --> 01:39:26.520
So we have partnered to create
what we call the Urban Design

01:39:26.520 --> 01:39:27.810
Corps.

01:39:27.810 --> 01:39:30.690
Just like the Peace Corps
strove to provide basic needs

01:39:30.690 --> 01:39:35.740
and things for people who
didn't have food or clothing

01:39:35.740 --> 01:39:38.960
and shelter, we hope to
build on the internet what

01:39:38.960 --> 01:39:42.390
we call the Urban Design Corps,
a place where we will teach

01:39:42.390 --> 01:39:47.130
people to create, where they
can take their creations

01:39:47.130 --> 01:39:50.610
and post them where businesses
can then take those creations

01:39:50.610 --> 01:39:53.820
and employ people
and create jobs.

01:39:53.820 --> 01:39:55.830
We're going to harness
the power of creativity,

01:39:55.830 --> 01:39:58.020
we believe, throughout
the world to help

01:39:58.020 --> 01:39:59.550
all people lift themselves up.

01:39:59.550 --> 01:40:04.350
That is our dream, and
that dream is kind of

01:40:04.350 --> 01:40:06.750
inspired by Dr. King,
so we have a little poem

01:40:06.750 --> 01:40:08.190
that we wrote for him too.

01:40:08.190 --> 01:40:10.550
And I'll finish with that
because I'm only allowed

01:40:10.550 --> 01:40:12.150
I think 47 more seconds.

01:40:12.150 --> 01:40:15.272
[CHUCKLING]

01:40:16.170 --> 01:40:19.050
His dream was dedicated
to our parents.

01:40:19.050 --> 01:40:20.550
They gave us life.

01:40:20.550 --> 01:40:22.670
They shielded us from strife.

01:40:22.670 --> 01:40:26.250
They are why we must carry on.

01:40:26.250 --> 01:40:30.540
His dream was dedicated
to our everlasting souls.

01:40:30.540 --> 01:40:33.210
This is why we will
work into the night.

01:40:33.210 --> 01:40:36.120
This is why we will
work for what is right.

01:40:36.120 --> 01:40:40.050
This is what bring
us into God's fold.

01:40:40.050 --> 01:40:44.020
His dream was dedicated
to our children.

01:40:44.020 --> 01:40:47.040
They renew the life within us.

01:40:47.040 --> 01:40:49.890
They drive our problems from us.

01:40:49.890 --> 01:40:54.900
They are why we must
stay on the right path.

01:40:54.900 --> 01:40:58.050
His dream was dedicated to
the Almighty, who gave us

01:40:58.050 --> 01:41:02.880
ambition and drive, who gave
us the ability to create,

01:41:02.880 --> 01:41:08.180
not stagnate, who we
will not let down.

01:41:08.180 --> 01:41:08.680
Thank you.

01:41:08.680 --> 01:41:12.110
[APPLAUSE]

01:41:19.480 --> 01:41:21.661
Can I give this to Mr. Mfume?

01:41:21.661 --> 01:41:22.811
BACHAU: Absolutely.

01:41:22.811 --> 01:41:24.310
SLOCUM: Should I
do the one picture?

01:41:24.310 --> 01:41:25.460
OK.

01:41:25.460 --> 01:41:27.750
Mr. Mfume, can you
come back up, please?

01:41:27.750 --> 01:41:28.818
Pretty please?

01:41:28.818 --> 01:41:32.304
[CHUCKLING]

01:41:36.300 --> 01:41:39.540
What I would like
to do is present you

01:41:39.540 --> 01:41:44.350
with what we think will be our
kickoff CD for the Urban Design

01:41:44.350 --> 01:41:44.850
Corps.

01:41:44.850 --> 01:41:47.490
It's called "The Journey
of the Lost Souls,"

01:41:47.490 --> 01:41:50.590
and it was written
by Mark Graham.

01:41:50.590 --> 01:41:53.390
And we actually
have a tape, too,

01:41:53.390 --> 01:41:56.750
so you can listen to
this on the way back.

01:41:56.750 --> 01:41:59.220
And the poem for your view.

01:41:59.220 --> 01:42:02.324
And then another
fun student I've

01:42:02.324 --> 01:42:05.610
had the privilege of knowing--

01:42:05.610 --> 01:42:08.070
[INAUDIBLE],, are
you around anywhere?

01:42:08.070 --> 01:42:09.555
He here today?

01:42:09.555 --> 01:42:10.550
MFUME: There he is.

01:42:10.550 --> 01:42:13.950
SLOCUM: He created
this fantastic poster,

01:42:13.950 --> 01:42:16.830
and we've inscribed on it "The
Journey of the Lost Souls"

01:42:16.830 --> 01:42:18.300
poem.

01:42:18.300 --> 01:42:21.540
And I think it
very clearly shows

01:42:21.540 --> 01:42:23.030
what path we want to create.

01:42:23.030 --> 01:42:24.780
And I think, [INAUDIBLE],,
the big version

01:42:24.780 --> 01:42:27.988
of it you have out on
display in lobby 7, right?

01:42:27.988 --> 01:42:29.181
[INAUDIBLE]

01:42:29.181 --> 01:42:29.680
Oh.

01:42:29.680 --> 01:42:31.160
[CHUCKLING]

01:42:31.160 --> 01:42:32.672
Well, we'll find it.

01:42:32.672 --> 01:42:33.664
Thank you.

01:42:33.664 --> 01:42:35.152
MFUME: Thank you.

01:42:35.152 --> 01:42:36.144
SLOCUM: For you, sir.

01:42:36.144 --> 01:42:37.136
MFUME: Thank you.

01:42:37.136 --> 01:42:40.608
[APPLAUSE]

01:42:50.530 --> 01:42:52.900
BACHAU: Thank you,
Alex, for being Alex.

01:42:52.900 --> 01:42:55.744
[CHUCKLING]

01:42:57.640 --> 01:43:00.451
Our next honoree is
Richard Williamson.

01:43:00.451 --> 01:43:01.924
Richard, would you
please come up?

01:43:01.924 --> 01:43:05.361
[APPLAUSE]

01:43:10.780 --> 01:43:14.781
Richard is an alumnus,
MIT class of 1987.

01:43:14.781 --> 01:43:15.280
Richard--

01:43:15.280 --> 01:43:15.946
WILLIAMSON: '85.

01:43:15.946 --> 01:43:16.900
BACHAU: '85.

01:43:16.900 --> 01:43:18.600
My instructions were wrong.

01:43:18.600 --> 01:43:20.620
1985.

01:43:20.620 --> 01:43:25.750
Richard has chosen a relatively
unusual path for an MIT

01:43:25.750 --> 01:43:28.360
alumnus, and that is
that most of the world

01:43:28.360 --> 01:43:29.830
tends to think of us--

01:43:29.830 --> 01:43:33.070
and I'm an alumnus as
well, so I can say us--

01:43:33.070 --> 01:43:39.190
as engineers, as
scientists, as geeks.

01:43:39.190 --> 01:43:40.930
Richard, however,
has made his way

01:43:40.930 --> 01:43:43.210
in the world in a different way.

01:43:43.210 --> 01:43:46.450
He is a teacher in the
Boston public schools,

01:43:46.450 --> 01:43:48.940
and not just any
kind of a teacher.

01:43:48.940 --> 01:43:52.090
Richard teaches at an
alternative school,

01:43:52.090 --> 01:43:54.220
a school of his
choosing, a school which

01:43:54.220 --> 01:43:58.750
is for young men and women
who have been expelled

01:43:58.750 --> 01:44:00.940
from the Boston public schools.

01:44:00.940 --> 01:44:03.490
Richard has chosen a
path where he can really

01:44:03.490 --> 01:44:05.740
have an influence on
the lives of people

01:44:05.740 --> 01:44:10.900
who are, in many cases,
most in need of inspiration,

01:44:10.900 --> 01:44:13.810
of leadership, of
support, and he

01:44:13.810 --> 01:44:17.830
has done so in a way that
has touched the lives of many

01:44:17.830 --> 01:44:19.510
of Boston's youth.

01:44:19.510 --> 01:44:23.380
In addition to his
activities as a teacher

01:44:23.380 --> 01:44:24.924
in the Boston public schools--

01:44:24.924 --> 01:44:26.590
I have to pull out
Richard's card, which

01:44:26.590 --> 01:44:28.480
I think is wonderful--

01:44:28.480 --> 01:44:30.970
Richard has also formed
another organization

01:44:30.970 --> 01:44:34.060
to help Boston's youth
which has the absolutely

01:44:34.060 --> 01:44:40.426
wonderful and inspiring name
of God's Posse Incorporated.

01:44:40.426 --> 01:44:43.800
[APPLAUSE]

01:44:48.630 --> 01:44:54.810
I read to you from the letter of
award from Dr. Vest to Richard.

01:44:54.810 --> 01:44:58.950
In his letter of nomination,
Dr. Ramon E. Samuel of Brigham

01:44:58.950 --> 01:45:02.190
and Women's Hospital cited
your unwavering commitment

01:45:02.190 --> 01:45:05.370
and selfless service to young
people in the neighborhoods

01:45:05.370 --> 01:45:07.590
of Roxbury and Dorchester.

01:45:07.590 --> 01:45:12.060
Your efforts as a teacher,
mentor, and community leader

01:45:12.060 --> 01:45:13.800
have brought hope
and inspiration

01:45:13.800 --> 01:45:16.500
to the lives of promising
young people who

01:45:16.500 --> 01:45:22.380
otherwise may have been lost,
neglected, or forgotten.

01:45:22.380 --> 01:45:25.380
Your outstanding work
and strong contributions

01:45:25.380 --> 01:45:28.170
are a fitting tribute to
the legacy of Dr. King

01:45:28.170 --> 01:45:32.460
and to all who passionately
believe in the great tradition

01:45:32.460 --> 01:45:35.040
of education and service.

01:45:35.040 --> 01:45:38.310
Richard, in giving
you this award today,

01:45:38.310 --> 01:45:41.040
I want you to know
that through your work,

01:45:41.040 --> 01:45:46.110
you honor all of MIT, its
alumni, and all the people that

01:45:46.110 --> 01:45:47.994
serve our great community.

01:45:47.994 --> 01:45:48.982
Congratulations.

01:45:48.982 --> 01:45:52.440
[APPLAUSE]

01:46:24.120 --> 01:46:27.120
WILLIAMSON: I'm reminded
of the preacher that

01:46:27.120 --> 01:46:32.220
said when Jesus was coming
into the town of Jerusalem

01:46:32.220 --> 01:46:35.070
and the crowds were
cheering and worshipping him

01:46:35.070 --> 01:46:39.630
that the donkey realized that
the praise was for the Lord

01:46:39.630 --> 01:46:44.840
and not for him, and I
feel like that donkey.

01:46:44.840 --> 01:46:47.875
Scriptures teach to whom much
is given, much is required.

01:46:47.875 --> 01:46:50.250
And I felt that I'd been one
who's been blessed and given

01:46:50.250 --> 01:46:52.830
much, and so I felt
it my obligation

01:46:52.830 --> 01:46:55.980
to seek a way to give
back, not knowing how, not

01:46:55.980 --> 01:46:58.540
knowing that path to take.

01:46:58.540 --> 01:47:00.480
But I had the opportunity
to come to MIT

01:47:00.480 --> 01:47:03.240
and to be taught by
this illustrious faculty

01:47:03.240 --> 01:47:06.300
and to learn some things that
challenge me and taught me

01:47:06.300 --> 01:47:11.070
that anything is possible
if you work hard at it.

01:47:11.070 --> 01:47:12.630
And God opened up
doors for me to be

01:47:12.630 --> 01:47:16.860
able to leave here and go
into the Roxbury community

01:47:16.860 --> 01:47:20.280
and work with young men,
particularly young men who

01:47:20.280 --> 01:47:23.280
are considered the
throwaways, the castaways,

01:47:23.280 --> 01:47:26.760
the super-predators, those whom
the prisons are being built

01:47:26.760 --> 01:47:32.280
for, those who institutions
are not looking forward to them

01:47:32.280 --> 01:47:36.120
coming, and to see in them
the potential for our country,

01:47:36.120 --> 01:47:38.250
to see in them a
leadership, to see

01:47:38.250 --> 01:47:40.700
in them gifts and
talents and abilities,

01:47:40.700 --> 01:47:43.460
to see in them the gift of God.

01:47:43.460 --> 01:47:45.210
And God has privileged
me with the ability

01:47:45.210 --> 01:47:48.817
to be able to reach them,
teach them, and to lead them,

01:47:48.817 --> 01:47:51.150
and I thank God for that
opportunity and that privilege.

01:47:51.150 --> 01:47:54.930
I'd like to acknowledge this
morning also my co-founder, Mr.

01:47:54.930 --> 01:47:57.010
Chris Womack, who I work
with in God's Posse.

01:47:57.010 --> 01:47:58.051
Can you stand up, please?

01:47:58.051 --> 01:48:01.244
[APPLAUSE]

01:48:04.196 --> 01:48:06.380
And two young men
that I work with,

01:48:06.380 --> 01:48:08.840
Mr. Steven Maguire,
who's a member of God's

01:48:08.840 --> 01:48:12.860
Posse, and Mr.
Daniel Davis, who is

01:48:12.860 --> 01:48:19.650
a senior at Community Academy
in Roxbury where I work.

01:48:19.650 --> 01:48:23.570
Finally, I'd just like to say
Dr. King said a man is not

01:48:23.570 --> 01:48:27.860
worthy to live until he's found
something worth dying for,

01:48:27.860 --> 01:48:29.960
and I found in these
young men, young men

01:48:29.960 --> 01:48:32.882
in the streets of Boston,
something worth dying for.

01:48:32.882 --> 01:48:33.382
Thank you.

01:48:33.382 --> 01:48:36.819
[APPLAUSE]

01:48:56.960 --> 01:48:59.180
BACHAU: Thank you, Richard.

01:48:59.180 --> 01:49:01.880
Our third honoree, Adriana Ogi.

01:49:01.880 --> 01:49:04.320
Adriana, would you
please come up?

01:49:04.320 --> 01:49:07.680
[APPLAUSE]

01:49:13.930 --> 01:49:17.990
Now, I have to tell something
which most people in this room

01:49:17.990 --> 01:49:20.210
just know from having
been around this place.

01:49:20.210 --> 01:49:23.180
But for our visitors, I
have to share with you

01:49:23.180 --> 01:49:28.160
the fact that MIT students
probably work harder and longer

01:49:28.160 --> 01:49:30.860
than any other people
in any other job

01:49:30.860 --> 01:49:33.230
on the face of the planet.

01:49:33.230 --> 01:49:35.270
In fact, we have an
expression at MIT

01:49:35.270 --> 01:49:38.750
that if you can't get your
work done in 24 hours in a day,

01:49:38.750 --> 01:49:40.730
start working nights.

01:49:40.730 --> 01:49:42.950
So it's all the
more remarkable when

01:49:42.950 --> 01:49:45.200
one encounters a
student like Adriana,

01:49:45.200 --> 01:49:49.550
who, in spite of bearing the
burden of an extraordinarily

01:49:49.550 --> 01:49:55.310
challenging curriculum, in
spite of having accomplished

01:49:55.310 --> 01:49:58.460
academically at an
extraordinarily high level,

01:49:58.460 --> 01:50:01.070
still manages to
find time amidst all

01:50:01.070 --> 01:50:04.790
of her other activities
to serve her community

01:50:04.790 --> 01:50:08.720
and to do so with grace and
with honor and with dignity,

01:50:08.720 --> 01:50:11.060
and to find time for others
who are less fortunate

01:50:11.060 --> 01:50:14.060
and to find time to
pass on the tradition

01:50:14.060 --> 01:50:18.440
and inspired others so that
they may accomplish someday

01:50:18.440 --> 01:50:20.700
as she has done as well.

01:50:20.700 --> 01:50:24.470
I read to you from
Adriana's letter of award.

01:50:24.470 --> 01:50:28.000
In her letter of nomination,
Dean Anne Davis Shaw,

01:50:28.000 --> 01:50:30.770
advisor to the National Society
of Hispanic Professional

01:50:30.770 --> 01:50:34.370
Engineers, cited the
depth of your commitment

01:50:34.370 --> 01:50:37.790
to helping fellow students
in the MIT community.

01:50:37.790 --> 01:50:39.440
Your outstanding
work as a leader

01:50:39.440 --> 01:50:41.800
has motivated many
peers, and you

01:50:41.800 --> 01:50:43.820
have contributed
effectively to a range

01:50:43.820 --> 01:50:45.830
of student organizations.

01:50:45.830 --> 01:50:48.650
As a calculus
teacher's assistant

01:50:48.650 --> 01:50:52.070
with the Minority Introduction
to Engineering and Science

01:50:52.070 --> 01:50:52.830
program--

01:50:52.830 --> 01:50:56.870
MITES-- you inspired 30 talented
secondary school students

01:50:56.870 --> 01:50:59.900
to strive for higher
academic goals.

01:50:59.900 --> 01:51:02.120
Equally admirable
is your ability

01:51:02.120 --> 01:51:05.180
to be fully engaged in
your own scholarship

01:51:05.180 --> 01:51:09.050
while never losing sight
of the needs of others.

01:51:09.050 --> 01:51:10.820
The Martin Luther
King Jr. Committee

01:51:10.820 --> 01:51:14.480
believes that you represent
much of what Dr. King wanted

01:51:14.480 --> 01:51:16.490
for all youth in this society.

01:51:16.490 --> 01:51:19.910
Your dedication to his
vision is a fitting tribute

01:51:19.910 --> 01:51:21.290
to that legacy.

01:51:21.290 --> 01:51:25.580
Adriana, you represent
a community of scholars,

01:51:25.580 --> 01:51:29.810
a community of students who
themselves, each and every one,

01:51:29.810 --> 01:51:34.070
are extraordinary, and even
among this extraordinary group,

01:51:34.070 --> 01:51:35.222
you stand out.

01:51:35.222 --> 01:51:36.206
Congratulations.

01:51:36.206 --> 01:51:39.158
[APPLAUSE]

01:51:58.370 --> 01:52:00.140
OGI: Thank you.

01:52:00.140 --> 01:52:03.390
I am very honored to
receive this award,

01:52:03.390 --> 01:52:07.760
and I would like to thank
the MLK Committee and the OME

01:52:07.760 --> 01:52:11.000
staff for all of their
hard work and efforts

01:52:11.000 --> 01:52:14.300
in making this event a success.

01:52:14.300 --> 01:52:15.830
I never thought
that my name would

01:52:15.830 --> 01:52:19.700
be associated with such an
excellent individual as Dr.

01:52:19.700 --> 01:52:22.610
Martin Luther King,
Jr. When I think

01:52:22.610 --> 01:52:26.000
of Dr. King and the
motivation he possessed,

01:52:26.000 --> 01:52:28.520
it makes me ask
myself, what else can I

01:52:28.520 --> 01:52:32.030
do to become a better leader?

01:52:32.030 --> 01:52:35.390
Whether I become a
professor in the future

01:52:35.390 --> 01:52:38.360
or whether I decide
to work in industry,

01:52:38.360 --> 01:52:40.580
I always want to keep
Dr. Martin Luther King,

01:52:40.580 --> 01:52:44.330
Jr's characteristics in mind.

01:52:44.330 --> 01:52:46.070
During the years
when Dr. King was

01:52:46.070 --> 01:52:48.620
at the top of the
civil rights movement

01:52:48.620 --> 01:52:53.300
leading hundreds of people,
he never became power hungry.

01:52:53.300 --> 01:52:55.760
I deeply admire
him for that, and I

01:52:55.760 --> 01:52:58.610
pray that in times of
success and achievement,

01:52:58.610 --> 01:53:01.500
humility will not be forgotten.

01:53:01.500 --> 01:53:03.950
What I admire the
most about Dr. King

01:53:03.950 --> 01:53:07.310
is that God always
came first in his life.

01:53:07.310 --> 01:53:09.890
When there were struggles,
he would pray to God

01:53:09.890 --> 01:53:13.040
to give him strength
and patience.

01:53:13.040 --> 01:53:15.150
And I'm sure that when
things were going well,

01:53:15.150 --> 01:53:19.820
his first instinct was to
thank God for his blessings.

01:53:19.820 --> 01:53:24.140
These are things that I always
want to keep close to my heart.

01:53:24.140 --> 01:53:26.930
I accept this award
with gratitude.

01:53:26.930 --> 01:53:31.250
I also accept it as a challenge
to continue in my path

01:53:31.250 --> 01:53:35.900
to educate myself as well as
others, and at the same time,

01:53:35.900 --> 01:53:38.360
never forget the
important things in life--

01:53:38.360 --> 01:53:41.500
humility and faith in God.

01:53:41.500 --> 01:53:42.490
Thank you.

01:53:42.490 --> 01:53:45.955
[APPLAUSE]

01:54:03.320 --> 01:54:05.440
BACHAU: Before I turn
things back to Shana,

01:54:05.440 --> 01:54:09.980
I just wanted to add one
more comment and observation.

01:54:09.980 --> 01:54:13.490
I, too, would like to thank Mr.
Mfume for his truly inspiring

01:54:13.490 --> 01:54:20.330
remarks, which were
delivered with passion,

01:54:20.330 --> 01:54:24.740
with grace in a way that I
think everybody in this room

01:54:24.740 --> 01:54:26.780
was moved.

01:54:26.780 --> 01:54:29.930
We have a problem at MIT,
and that is getting students

01:54:29.930 --> 01:54:32.510
to come to classes at 9
o'clock in the morning,

01:54:32.510 --> 01:54:34.250
and I think we've
discovered a solution.

01:54:34.250 --> 01:54:36.620
And so I would like to
extend an invitation

01:54:36.620 --> 01:54:40.910
to Mfume that if he can hold a
crowd like this at this hour,

01:54:40.910 --> 01:54:43.140
we'd love to have
you teach physics.

01:54:43.140 --> 01:54:45.100
[CHUCKLING]

01:54:45.100 --> 01:54:46.080
Thank you very much.

01:54:46.080 --> 01:54:49.510
[APPLAUSE]

01:54:55.672 --> 01:54:57.630
SMITH: Thank you very
much, and congratulations

01:54:57.630 --> 01:54:59.670
to all the recipients.

01:54:59.670 --> 01:55:02.460
Now it's my pleasure to
introduce Provost Robert A.

01:55:02.460 --> 01:55:07.230
Brown, who will recognize the
1998-1999 Martin Luther King

01:55:07.230 --> 01:55:08.940
Jr. Visiting Professors.

01:55:08.940 --> 01:55:11.290
Provost Brown.

01:55:11.290 --> 01:55:14.580
[APPLAUSE]

01:55:16.470 --> 01:55:17.760
BROWN: Thank you, Shana.

01:55:17.760 --> 01:55:20.490
This morning has reminded me
of an extraordinary number

01:55:20.490 --> 01:55:22.860
of things, but I
think it's reminded us

01:55:22.860 --> 01:55:26.850
all of the commitment we
have to have of building

01:55:26.850 --> 01:55:29.250
an academic community
that exemplifies

01:55:29.250 --> 01:55:32.430
the path that Martin
Luther King, Jr. challenged

01:55:32.430 --> 01:55:34.420
us to follow.

01:55:34.420 --> 01:55:37.440
The Martin Luther King, Jr.
Visiting Professor Program

01:55:37.440 --> 01:55:42.030
began in 1994 to bring to
MIT outstanding scholars

01:55:42.030 --> 01:55:45.180
to participate in teaching
and research at the Institute

01:55:45.180 --> 01:55:47.520
and to enrich our community.

01:55:47.520 --> 01:55:50.160
Appointments to this
program are made

01:55:50.160 --> 01:55:55.110
upon recommendations from the
departments and to the provost.

01:55:55.110 --> 01:55:57.210
This year, there have
been five Martin Luther

01:55:57.210 --> 01:55:59.490
King, Jr. visiting professors.

01:55:59.490 --> 01:56:01.170
I'd like to take
this opportunity

01:56:01.170 --> 01:56:03.930
to recognize the four
individuals who are here

01:56:03.930 --> 01:56:07.620
this morning and to introduce
you, at least through my words,

01:56:07.620 --> 01:56:08.940
to the fifth.

01:56:08.940 --> 01:56:14.340
I'll ask each one to stand at
the table where I was seated

01:56:14.340 --> 01:56:16.576
and give you a brief
description of them.

01:56:16.576 --> 01:56:20.544
Dr. Lloyd Dimitrius.

01:56:20.544 --> 01:56:24.016
[APPLAUSE]

01:56:29.000 --> 01:56:31.280
Dr. Dimitrius is visiting
in the Department

01:56:31.280 --> 01:56:33.230
of Brain and Cognitive Science.

01:56:33.230 --> 01:56:36.260
He is with the Department of
Biology at Harvard University

01:56:36.260 --> 01:56:38.630
and is an expert in
mathematics and biophysics

01:56:38.630 --> 01:56:42.470
and in the wonderful world of
nonlinear dynamical systems.

01:56:42.470 --> 01:56:46.700
His research at MIT has spanned
across biology, chemistry,

01:56:46.700 --> 01:56:48.620
in brain and cognitive
science, and is now

01:56:48.620 --> 01:56:51.230
taking him into bioengineering
and chemical engineering

01:56:51.230 --> 01:56:52.940
this spring.

01:56:52.940 --> 01:56:57.140
His teaching has been in the
course evolutionary psychology

01:56:57.140 --> 01:57:00.710
in BSC, co-taught with
Professor Stephen Pinker,

01:57:00.710 --> 01:57:03.500
truly integrated
in the community.

01:57:03.500 --> 01:57:05.722
Let me introduce
Mr. Sterling Hunter.

01:57:05.722 --> 01:57:07.210
Sterling?

01:57:07.210 --> 01:57:10.682
[APPLAUSE]

01:57:16.650 --> 01:57:18.960
Mr. Hunter is a visiting
professor of management

01:57:18.960 --> 01:57:21.720
in the Sloan School, where
his research is focusing

01:57:21.720 --> 01:57:27.090
on organizational design and
organizational consequences

01:57:27.090 --> 01:57:28.830
of information theory.

01:57:28.830 --> 01:57:31.560
At MIT, Mr. Hunter
is also interested

01:57:31.560 --> 01:57:33.690
in the virtual organization.

01:57:33.690 --> 01:57:36.210
His teaching has been
in the graduate subject

01:57:36.210 --> 01:57:38.820
in strategic management.

01:57:38.820 --> 01:57:42.285
Let me introduce
Dr. Linda Jordan.

01:57:42.285 --> 01:57:42.785
Linda.

01:57:42.785 --> 01:57:45.743
[APPLAUSE]

01:57:51.680 --> 01:57:54.690
Dr. Jordan is visiting the
Department of Chemistry.

01:57:54.690 --> 01:57:56.390
She's currently an
associate professor

01:57:56.390 --> 01:57:59.030
in the Department of Chemistry
at North Carolina Agricultural

01:57:59.030 --> 01:58:02.030
and Technical State University.

01:58:02.030 --> 01:58:05.630
Her research is in the
area of human biochemistry

01:58:05.630 --> 01:58:09.320
and expertise is in enzymes,
especially in the enzyme that

01:58:09.320 --> 01:58:12.230
affects diseases in man,
including arthritis, asthma,

01:58:12.230 --> 01:58:14.540
pulmonary disorders, and cancer.

01:58:14.540 --> 01:58:17.270
At MIT, she's
focused on research,

01:58:17.270 --> 01:58:19.460
along with
undergraduate students,

01:58:19.460 --> 01:58:24.752
on cloning a specific
enzyme and examining

01:58:24.752 --> 01:58:25.835
its structural properties.

01:58:25.835 --> 01:58:28.190
Dr. [INAUDIBLE]

01:58:28.190 --> 01:58:32.423
Let me introduce Dr.
Pamela McCarley Bell.

01:58:32.423 --> 01:58:33.866
Pamela.

01:58:33.866 --> 01:58:36.752
[APPLAUSE]

01:58:40.610 --> 01:58:43.160
Pamela is visiting in the
Department of Aeronautics

01:58:43.160 --> 01:58:44.570
and Astronautics.

01:58:44.570 --> 01:58:46.340
She's currently an
associate professor

01:58:46.340 --> 01:58:48.110
in industrial engineering
and management

01:58:48.110 --> 01:58:51.680
systems in the University
of Central Florida.

01:58:51.680 --> 01:58:54.440
Her research focuses
on the development

01:58:54.440 --> 01:58:56.720
of intelligent software
and predictive models

01:58:56.720 --> 01:58:59.510
to address information
warfare, information security,

01:58:59.510 --> 01:59:02.660
and human factors problems.

01:59:02.660 --> 01:59:05.750
She's worked here in the field
of human impact of information

01:59:05.750 --> 01:59:08.480
security, where she's
brought her expertise

01:59:08.480 --> 01:59:11.697
to a range of groups in
the aero-astro department,

01:59:11.697 --> 01:59:14.030
including the human factors
groups and the group focused

01:59:14.030 --> 01:59:15.680
on intelligence systems.

01:59:15.680 --> 01:59:18.620
She's been teaching a seminar
on information security,

01:59:18.620 --> 01:59:21.050
and she's served as the advisor
to seven freshmen and two

01:59:21.050 --> 01:59:23.720
graduate students while here.

01:59:23.720 --> 01:59:27.800
Not with us today is
Dr. Arnold Stancel,

01:59:27.800 --> 01:59:30.410
who visited this fall in
the Department of Chemical

01:59:30.410 --> 01:59:31.460
Engineering.

01:59:31.460 --> 01:59:35.120
He's a professor
currently at the Georgia

01:59:35.120 --> 01:59:37.850
Institute of Technology and
has returned this spring

01:59:37.850 --> 01:59:39.080
to teach there.

01:59:39.080 --> 01:59:43.340
While at MIT, he brought to
bear his enormous experience.

01:59:43.340 --> 01:59:46.910
Arnie spent 31 years at
Mobil before coming back

01:59:46.910 --> 01:59:50.390
into the academic profession.

01:59:50.390 --> 01:59:53.360
While here, he consulted on
energy issues in the Energy

01:59:53.360 --> 01:59:55.910
Lab, helped supervise
graduate students,

01:59:55.910 --> 01:59:58.010
and taught in a core
thermodynamics course

01:59:58.010 --> 01:59:59.750
in chemical engineering.

01:59:59.750 --> 02:00:03.050
Each of these individuals
has enriched the environment

02:00:03.050 --> 02:00:05.730
in MIT enormously
over their time here,

02:00:05.730 --> 02:00:07.790
and we look forward
to, in many cases,

02:00:07.790 --> 02:00:10.061
the time they have left with us.

02:00:10.061 --> 02:00:11.023
Thank you.

02:00:11.023 --> 02:00:14.871
[APPLAUSE]

02:00:22.590 --> 02:00:27.400
SMITH: Thank you, Provost Brown,
for recognizing our faculty.

02:00:27.400 --> 02:00:29.290
We will now continue
with some announcements

02:00:29.290 --> 02:00:32.950
that are relevant to this
morning's celebration.

02:00:32.950 --> 02:00:34.930
First, there will
be an installation

02:00:34.930 --> 02:00:38.890
of the Martin Luther King,
Jr. Design Project in Lobby 7

02:00:38.890 --> 02:00:41.410
at 11:00 AM.

02:00:41.410 --> 02:00:46.960
[INAUDIBLE] will be performing
at noon in Lobby 7 also.

02:00:46.960 --> 02:00:48.820
There will be a
conference called

02:00:48.820 --> 02:00:51.040
"Sisters in Cyberspace
Youth Conference"

02:00:51.040 --> 02:00:55.400
held here at the
Institute on February 6.

02:00:55.400 --> 02:00:57.250
Finally, they will--
oh, excuse me there.

02:00:57.250 --> 02:00:59.170
Fourthly, there will
be a presentation

02:00:59.170 --> 02:01:00.670
in the Black
Student Union, which

02:01:00.670 --> 02:01:03.490
is right through those doors,
hosted by the Installation

02:01:03.490 --> 02:01:07.150
Committee from 4 to 5 PM today.

02:01:07.150 --> 02:01:11.140
Finally, the South Mass Choir
will be performing in Lobby 7

02:01:11.140 --> 02:01:13.150
also at 5 PM.

02:01:13.150 --> 02:01:16.370
So I hope you all can
attend those events.

02:01:16.370 --> 02:01:20.140
Now I'd like to introduce
Champlain Betsy Draper, who

02:01:20.140 --> 02:01:23.200
will offer the benediction for
the close of this morning's

02:01:23.200 --> 02:01:24.610
program.

02:01:24.610 --> 02:01:25.390
Champlain Draper?

02:01:54.602 --> 02:01:56.435
DRAPER: May everyone
stand together, please?

02:02:03.460 --> 02:02:04.120
May we pray.

02:02:07.970 --> 02:02:13.186
Oh, God and father of humankind,
forgive our foolish ways.

02:02:18.110 --> 02:02:24.890
May our renewal today starve the
beast of racism and favoritism,

02:02:24.890 --> 02:02:30.260
never to devour the
dream of justice for all.

02:02:30.260 --> 02:02:34.940
May our actions, our
conduct, our words, our deeds

02:02:34.940 --> 02:02:37.340
nourish the dream and to
the reality of harmony.

02:02:40.100 --> 02:02:46.220
Today, we ask for courage to
serve and to continue to serve,

02:02:46.220 --> 02:02:50.150
to never give up as
components of change

02:02:50.150 --> 02:02:54.710
for good in an
ever-changing world.

02:02:54.710 --> 02:02:57.050
We ask for direction.

02:02:57.050 --> 02:02:59.470
We ask for your
sustaining presence

02:02:59.470 --> 02:03:03.140
as we rally round the
standard of blind equality,

02:03:03.140 --> 02:03:08.195
leaving behind in the pit
our desires for blind power

02:03:08.195 --> 02:03:08.695
and pride.

02:03:11.630 --> 02:03:16.630
Oh, God and father
of humankind, we

02:03:16.630 --> 02:03:20.380
look forward to a better day.

02:03:20.380 --> 02:03:22.670
We ask these things.

02:03:22.670 --> 02:03:23.170
Amen.

02:03:33.802 --> 02:03:36.260
SMITH: Well, originally we had
scheduled for the MIT Gospel

02:03:36.260 --> 02:03:39.140
Choir to sing, but
now we are going

02:03:39.140 --> 02:03:42.500
to move on to just a closing.

02:03:49.080 --> 02:03:51.257
I hope-- you may all be seated.

02:03:51.257 --> 02:03:51.840
You may all be

02:03:51.840 --> 02:03:52.340
Seated.

02:03:56.330 --> 02:03:59.210
I hope you all have enjoyed
this morning's program as much

02:03:59.210 --> 02:04:03.860
as I have and have taken a
moment to reflect and remember

02:04:03.860 --> 02:04:07.910
Dr. King and the life and the
legacy that he has left behind.

02:04:07.910 --> 02:04:10.040
Thank you all for
coming, and of course, we

02:04:10.040 --> 02:04:11.600
hope to see you next year.

02:04:11.600 --> 02:04:15.020
And this concludes the 25th
Annual Martin Luther King Jr.

02:04:15.020 --> 02:04:15.920
Celebration.

02:04:15.920 --> 02:04:16.520
Thank you.

02:04:16.520 --> 02:04:19.870
[APPLAUSE]

